WEBVTT

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:44.270
<v Ira Glass>OK. So here's a story you probably heard and a story you have not. Remember in 2018 when Hawaiians got that alert on their phones saying that a ballistic missile was inbound, headed for Hawaii, this is not a drill, seek immediate shelter. People scrambled to find places to hide. They put their kids in storm drains. It seemed completely plausible that a missile was on the way because this was after months of President Trump and North Korea's leader Kim Jong-un threatening each other with nuclear destruction, fire, and fury. OK, so that is the story you know. Here's the one you don't.
<v Ira Glass>好的，接下来的故事你可能听过，也可能没听过。还记得2018年吗，当时夏威夷人的手机上收到了一条警报，说有一枚弹道导弹正在飞向夏威夷，这不是演习，请立即寻找避难所。人们争先恐后地寻找躲藏的地方，甚至把孩子塞进下水道。导弹来袭看起来完全真实可信，因为那是在特朗普总统和朝鲜领导人金正恩互相威胁要以核毁灭、战火和愤怒摧毁对方之后的几个月。好的，这就是你们知道的故事。接下来是你们不知道的那个。

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:55.280
<v Mike McFarland>When the alert came, I was at my house up on my lawn. My first thought was, wow, I'm going to view history and then get wiped off the map.
<v Mike McFarland>警报响起时，我正在家里的草坪上。我第一个念头是：哇，我即将见证历史，然后从地图上被抹去。

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:22.820
<v Ira Glass>This is Mike McFarland. And he figured he'd be viewing history because his lawn is in a part of Oahu that overlooks Pearl Harbor, which is where he figured the missile was going to land. There's still an active military base there. So he grabs his cat to bring her inside, flips on TV and radio. The same alert, "Incoming missile," is everywhere, which makes it more real and more terrifying. He props up an iPad at a window and points it at Pearl Harbor to live stream whatever's about to happen, which he admits--
<v Ira Glass>这位是麦克·麦克法兰。他觉得自己会见证历史，因为他的草坪位于瓦胡岛上，可以俯瞰珍珠港，他认为导弹会落在那儿。那里现在还有一个现役军事基地。于是，他抓起猫带进屋，打开电视和收音机。同样的“导弹来袭”警报到处都是，这让情况变得更加真实，也更加恐怖。他在窗边架起一台iPad，对着珍珠港进行直播，准备记录即将发生的一切，对此他承认——

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:34.100
<v Mike McFarland>Like, I'm not rational. I just got the news my world was going to end. Now I have about 15 minutes to live, in my mind. And--
<v Mike McFarland>就像，我不理智了。我刚收到消息说我的世界要终结了。现在我心想，我大概还有15分钟活头。而且——

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:42.200
<v Ira Glass>He starts thinking about his life and his grandfather, who died when he was little, and some whiskey of his grandfather's that he still had somewhere.
<v Ira Glass>他开始想起自己的人生，想起他小时候就去世的祖父，以及他依然藏在某处的祖父留下的威士忌。

00:01:42.200 --> 00:02:19.130
<v Mike McFarland>--grab. I found this thing. It's been up in the closet. And it's the beautiful bottle. And I took a big drink. And I toasted my grandfather and said, OK, goodbye. This is it. And then, I read this article once, that how you survive a nuclear blast. And it just kind of came out of nowhere from the dark reaches of my mind. It's when the nuclear thing comes, you're supposed to go in a bathtub and cover yourself up with a blanket. So I took my cat in there. I had my phone in there, cover myself up, and just laid there, and just waited. And--
<v Mike McFarland>——抓起（酒）。我发现了它。它一直放在壁橱里。那是一个漂亮的瓶子。我喝了一大口，向我的祖父敬酒说，好吧，再见。就是这样了。然后，我读过一篇文章，讲如何在核爆炸中幸存。它不知怎么地从我记忆的深处冒了出来。就是当核打击到来时，你应该钻进浴缸，用毯子盖住自己。所以我把猫带了进去。我带着手机钻进去，盖上毯子，躺在那里，等待着。而且——

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:29.510
<v Ira Glass>By the way, and no offense to Mike, going into your bathtub will not protect you from a nuclear blast. Well, you know, it's funny where your mind goes when you think you just have minutes to live.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，对麦克没有冒犯的意思，躲进浴缸并不能保护你免受核爆炸的伤害。嗯，你知道，当你以为只剩下几分钟生命时，脑子里冒出的念头真是很有趣。

00:02:29.510 --> 00:03:01.850
<v Mike McFarland>And I think I had the moment that a lot of people think about-- but let me tell you it's different when it happens-- it's like, OK, I'm going to die. I'm going to die right here. And my life just sort of came rushing in front of my eyes. It's like, OK, what am I proud of? What am I sorry about? And I thought about Lynn. I thought about the last girlfriend I had. And it wasn't a pretty breakup. And I felt--
<v Mike McFarland>我想当时我确实有了那种很多人会想到的瞬间——但让我告诉你，当事情真的发生时感觉是不一样的——就像，好吧，我要死了。我就要死在这儿了。我的人生就像走马灯一样在我眼前闪过。心想，好吧，我为自己感到骄傲的是什么？我感到遗憾的是什么？我想到了琳。我想到了我的前女友。我们的分手并不体面。我感到——

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.030
<v Ira Glass>They'd broken up maybe six months before this, after a year together.
<v Ira Glass>他们在一起一年后，在这件事发生前大约六个月分了手。

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:22.160
<v Mike McFarland>And I thought, OK, well, I'll just send a text to Lynn and just tell her goodbye and tell her how important she was in my life and love of my life. And so I did. I sent that.
<v Mike McFarland>我想，好吧，我给琳发条短信说声再见吧，告诉她她在我的生命中有多重要，她是我一生挚爱。于是我照做了。我发出了那条短信。

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:55.790
<v Ira Glass>The exact words of the text-- "Lynn, I just got word that a nuclear missile on its way to Honolulu. I'm not sure where you are or if you'll even read this, but I thought of you. I wanted to let you know that looking back, you were the love of my life. If it is, in fact, over in a few minutes, thank you for the time we spent together." Then he lays there and waits. Before long-- 38 minutes after the original alert-- he and everybody else learned there's no incoming missile.
<v Ira Glass>短信的原文是——“琳，我刚得到消息说一枚核导弹正在飞往檀香山的路上。我不确定你在哪儿，甚至不知道你是否会读到这条短信，但我想到了你。我想让你知道，回首往事，你曾是我的一生挚爱。如果这一切真的在几分钟内结束，谢谢我们在一起的时光。”然后他躺在那里等待。不久之后——在最初的警报发出38分钟后——他和所有人都知道根本没有导弹来袭。

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:11.630
<v Mike McFarland>And I have this-- I don't know if I can use this word on your station but-- oh, blank moment. It's like, oh, this is really embarrassing. It's like, I'm not dead. And I sent this message.
<v Mike McFarland>而我当时有一种——我不知道能不能在你们电台上用这个词，但是——哦，空白时刻。就像，噢，这太尴尬了。就像，我没死。但我却发了那条信息。

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:26.810
<v Ira Glass>Lynn would not see this message for hours, because right when the alert came over, she was actually on the runway at Inouye Airport in Honolulu, on a flight headed to California to look at colleges with her son, who saw the first alert on his phone.
<v Ira Glass>琳几个小时后才看到这条信息，因为警报响起时，她正坐在檀香山井上机场的跑道上，准备飞往加州陪儿子看大学，儿子在手机上看到了第一条警报。

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:54.030
<v Lynn>He was terrified saying, my dad is dead, my friends are dead. I'm going to war, Mom. Our country is going to war. This is World War III. Ghost white, his hands were sweaty, and I'm consoling him. But in my mind, as an adult, I'm like, OK, pilots, just-- expletive-- take off. Just-- expletive- take off. Do not listen to air traffic control. Just go. Please, just go, plane.
<v Lynn>他吓坏了，说：爸爸死了，朋友们也死了。我要去打仗了，妈妈。我们国家要开战了。这就是第三次世界大战。他脸色惨白，手心出汗，我在安慰他。但在我心里，作为一个成年人，我想的是，好吧，飞行员们，快点——该死——起飞吧。就——该死——起飞吧。别管空中交通管制。直接走。拜托，飞机快走。

00:04:54.030 --> 00:05:03.010
<v Ira Glass>It's not till they landed five hours later that Lynn sees Mike's text. Today, she remembers just one sentence of that text, the key sentence.
<v Ira Glass>直到五小时后飞机降落，琳才看到麦克的短信。今天，她只记得那条短信里的一句话，最关键的一句话。

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.720
<v Lynn>It said, "You were the love of my life."
<v Lynn>上面写着：“你曾是我的一生挚爱。”

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.490
<v Ira Glass>And what did you think when you saw that?
<v Ira Glass>当你看到那句话时，你是怎么想的？

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:23.820
<v Lynn>I think that we ended our relationship, for me, probably a little bit too abruptly. And I felt that perhaps there were more things to explore.
<v Lynn>我认为我们结束那段关系，对我来说，可能有点太仓促了。我觉得也许还有更多事情值得探索。

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:43.340
<v Ira Glass>So she was glad to see it. She wrote him back, "Let's talk." And when Lynn got back to Hawaii, she and Mike met up, hung out a bunch of times to see how it felt. And then they got back together because of that text, sent from a bathtub, under a blanket, "You were the love of my life."
<v Ira Glass>所以她很高兴看到那条短信。她回信说：“我们谈谈吧。”当琳回到夏威夷后，她和麦克见了面，在一起待了好几次，感受一下那种感觉。然后他们因为那条从浴缸里、在毯子下发出的短信——“你曾是我的一生挚爱”——复合了。

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:48.140
<v Ira Glass>Do you think you ever would have said this to her if not for the incoming nuclear attack?
<v Ira Glass>你认为如果不是因为那次核袭击警报，你还会对她说出这些话吗？

00:05:48.140 --> 00:06:04.700
<v Mike McFarland>Oh, absolutely not. Absolutely not. At that moment, in that moment, I was truly-- it changed for me.
<v Mike McFarland>噢，绝对不会。绝对不会。在那个时刻，在那一刻，我确实——那对我来说是一种改变。

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:26.390
<v Ira Glass>It was nicer this time, being together. They were better friends, better at talking through problems. They built a massive garden together, raised nasturtiums and tons of vegetables, took care of animals at an animal rescue place. But they had their differences, and it didn't last. After maybe six months, they decided to split up on friendly terms. Lynn says she's glad they tried.
<v Ira Glass>这次在一起更好了。他们成了更好的朋友，更擅长沟通解决问题。他们一起建了一个巨大的花园，种了旱金莲和成堆的蔬菜，还在动物救援中心照顾动物。但他们有分歧，这段感情没能持续下去。大约六个月后，他们决定友好分手。琳说她很高兴他们尝试过。

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:32.690
<v Lynn>I would say I was definitely glad for the chance for us to give it one more shot.
<v Lynn>我会说我绝对很高兴我们有机会再试一次。

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.970
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, it's like you got to run the science experiment again just to be sure you got the same result.
<v Ira Glass>是的，就像是你必须重新运行科学实验才能确定得到相同的结果一样。

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:40.680
<v Lynn>[LAUGHS] I suppose.
<v Lynn>[笑] 我想是吧。

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:51.460
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you, do you think he ever would have reached out if he didn't think he was going to die in a nuclear attack?
<v Ira Glass>我可以问你吗，你觉得如果他没有认为自己要在核袭击中死去，他还会联系你吗？

00:06:51.460 --> 00:07:06.410
<v Lynn>I-- wow. Hmm. I don't know. But I may have reached out to him. But, wow, that's a great question.
<v Lynn>我——哇。嗯。我不知道。但我可能会联系他。但是，哇，这是一个好问题。

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.640
<v Ira Glass>I actually asked him. Do you want to know what he said?
<v Ira Glass>我其实问过他。你想知道他是怎么说的吗？

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.440
<v Lynn>Yeah.
<v Lynn>想。

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:16.170
<v Ira Glass>He said, absolutely, he never would have texted you that if he didn't think he was going to die.
<v Ira Glass>他说，绝对不会，如果他不觉得自己要死了，他绝不会给你发那样的短信。

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:41.980
<v Lynn>Oh, wow. OK. It makes you wonder if perhaps those words weren't real, "You were the love of my life." I don't think that if someone is the love of your life that you just let it go. So perhaps he just did it in the spur of the moment.
<v Lynn>噢，哇。好吧。这让人不禁怀疑那句话也许不是真心的，“你曾是我的一生挚爱。”我不认为如果某人是你的一生挚爱，你会轻易放手。所以也许他只是在那一瞬间冲动之下才这么做的。

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:53.580
<v Ira Glass>Mike says no. He says he meant what he said. It was real. He just figured they were done before that moment came that accidentally ended up bringing them back together.
<v Ira Glass>麦克说不是。他说他是真心的。那是真实的。他只是觉得在那个导致他们复合的意外瞬间到来之前，他们就已经结束了。

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:59.090
<v Ira Glass>It's so interesting to think about, there really is nothing like a deadline.
<v Ira Glass>思考这件事很有趣，确实没有什么比截止日期更能促成事情了。

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:38.610
<v Mike McFarland>Exactly, and you can miss a deadline and get fired or whatever. But this deadline, I was going to die, right? I mean, it was the ultimate deadline, I guess, is the best way to say that. Right? It was the ultimate deadline. And I wish, going forward, there was a way for everybody to plug into that moment where, OK, you've got 15 minutes to truly fix things.
<v Mike McFarland>没错，你可能错过截止日期然后被解雇或者其他什么。但这个截止日期，我要死了，对吧？我的意思是，这是终极截止日期，我觉得这是最好的说法。对吧？这是终极截止日期。我希望以后能有一种方式让每个人都能接入那个瞬间，让你意识到，好吧，你还有15分钟来真正修复一切。

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:56.190
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, people trying to fix things at the very last minute. It is the way that so many things happen, too many things. Just this week, it seemed like the United States was going to miss one of its very last chances to get anywhere near the 2030 climate goals to hold the Earth's temperature rise at 1.5 degrees Celsius.
<v Ira Glass>那么，今天在我们的节目中，我们要讲那些在最后一刻试图修复问题的人们。这也是很多事情发生的方式，太多事情了。就在本周，美国似乎要错过实现2030年气候目标（将地球升温控制在1.5摄氏度以内）的最后机会之一了。

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:26.240
<v Ira Glass>And then, not even at the 11th hour-- it was after the clock had run out-- Senator Joe Manchin did a turnaround and agreed to legislation that would cut emissions. Why is it always like this? Today on our program, we observe the power of a crushing impeding deadline to focus the mind and lead to action. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>然后，甚至不是在最后一小时——而是在时钟已经走完之后——乔·曼钦参议员突然转变立场，同意了一项旨在削减排放的立法。为什么总是这样？今天在我们的节目中，我们要探讨迫在眉睫的最后期限如何聚焦思维并促成行动。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.230
<v Ira Glass>Act One, A Funny Thing Happened on the Way to the Quorum.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：通往法定人数之路的趣事。

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:47.960
<v Ira Glass>So, a bunch of people in a small town, Croydon, New Hampshire, population 800, wake up one morning not with an imaginary missile heading towards them, but something that felt, I don't know, dire, with a deadline they never saw coming. Sarah Gibson of New Hampshire Public Radio tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>一群人居住在新罕布什尔州的一个小镇克罗伊登，人口800人。一天早上醒来，迎面而来的不是虚构的导弹，而是某种让我感到，怎么说呢，可怕的事情，伴随着一个他们从未预料到的截止日期。来自新罕布什尔州公共广播电台的萨拉·吉布森讲述了发生了什么。

00:09:47.960 --> 00:10:07.760
<v Sarah Gibson>This story starts at a school budget meeting. In Croydon, barely anyone goes to these. Even the local TV guy, who sometimes films town meetings, did not show up this year. So there's not even a recording. But I'm going to tell you about it, because what happened that day triggered a very strange series of events.
<v Sarah Gibson>故事始于一次学校预算会议。在克罗伊登，几乎没人参加这种会议。甚至连那个有时会拍摄镇会议的本地电视工作者今年也没现身。所以甚至连录音都没有。但我会告诉你发生了什么，因为那天发生的事引发了一系列非常奇怪的事件。

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:22.740
<v Sarah Gibson>The meeting takes place at the Croydon Town Hall, an old, white building with tall windows and a big, drafty room. There's an American flag from the 1800s framed on the wall, some built-in pews, and a bunch of folding chairs. Tom Moore walks in.
<v Sarah Gibson>会议在克罗伊登市政厅举行，那是一栋古老的白色建筑，有着高大的窗户和一个空旷通风的房间。墙上装裱着一面1800年代的美国国旗，有一些嵌入式的长椅和一堆折叠椅。汤姆·摩尔走了进去。

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:40.490
<v Sarah Gibson>He's 43 years old, big, red beard, teaches high school math and woodworking, and has three little kids. Tom's been on the Croydon School Board for years. But this it's his last day. Doesn't expect anything dramatic to happen. He'd carved some wooden pens as goodbye gifts for his colleagues on the board.
<v Sarah Gibson>他43岁，留着浓密的红胡子，教高中数学和木工，有三个小孩。汤姆在克罗伊登学校董事会任职多年。但这是他的最后一天。没料到会发生什么戏剧性的事。他雕刻了一些木笔作为给同事们的告别礼物。

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:49.080
<v Tom Moore>And, as I walk in, I register my name and I get handed a pamphlet. And I don't really glance at it.
<v Tom Moore>当我走进去时，我登记了名字，领到了一份小册子。我并没有仔细看它。

00:10:49.080 --> 00:11:11.930
<v Sarah Gibson>He sits down. Everything seems normal. One of the townspeople in the audience is Jim Peschke, who knows this is not going to be normal. Jim is kind of a budget nerd, obsessed with the school budget. It's the town's biggest expense by a lot. This year, it's $1.7 million, the highest it's ever been. And every year, Jim comes in with a proposal to shrink it.
<v Sarah Gibson>他坐了下来。一切看起来都很正常。听众中的镇民之一吉姆·佩什克知道这不会正常。吉姆是个“预算书呆子”，痴迷于学校预算。这是小镇最大的开支，而且高出很多。今年是170万美元，是有史以来最高的。每年，吉姆都会带着一个削减预算的提案前来。

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:20.690
<v Jim Peschke>I, in years past, believe I've brought up $20,000 cuts that weren't approved because they were barbaric, in the words of the opponents.
<v Jim Peschke>过去几年，我认为我曾提出过2万美元的削减额，但没被批准，因为反对者说这太野蛮了。

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:35.390
<v Sarah Gibson>Jim's an electrical engineer, a transplant from the Midwest. He and his wife pride themselves on having homeschooled their kids rather than send them to public school. A thing to know about this budget meeting-- it's not just to talk about the proposed school budget.
<v Sarah Gibson>吉姆是一名电气工程师，从中西部搬来。他和妻子对让孩子在家教育而不是送去公立学校感到自豪。关于这个预算会议有一点要了解——它不仅仅是讨论拟议的学校预算。

00:11:35.390 --> 00:12:02.840
<v Sarah Gibson>The way things work here is that whoever shows up gets to actually vote on what the budget should be. This has been the approach in Croydon-- really most of small town New Hampshire-- since the 1800s. It's a relic that most people no longer participate in. Today at the town hall attendance is low, about 40 people. The meeting starts and Tom finally looks down at that pamphlet. The title is Budget or Ransom?
<v Sarah Gibson>这里的运作方式是，任何到场的人都可以实际投票决定预算应该是多少。这是克罗伊登——实际上是新罕布什尔州大多数小镇——自1800年代以来的做法。这是一个大多数人不再参与的遗迹。今天在市政厅，出席人数很少，大约40人。会议开始，汤姆终于看了那份小册子。标题是《预算还是勒索？》。

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:27.230
<v Tom Moore>And I go, oh, what is this? This is crazy. But as I'm looking at that, people are starting to get really upset about how much we're paying the principal and the superintendent, and about violins and snowshoes. And there just seems to be a lot of people up in arms.
<v Tom Moore>我说，噢，这是什么？这太疯狂了。但当我在看那个时，人们开始对我们支付给校长和督导的薪水，以及关于小提琴和雪鞋的开支感到非常愤怒。似乎很多人都准备抗争。

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:35.610
<v Sarah Gibson>The snow shoes and violins, paid for entirely with grants. But to some people here, it felt extravagant and wasteful.
<v Sarah Gibson>小提琴和雪鞋的费用完全是由补助金支付的。但对这里的一些人来说，这感觉像是挥霍和浪费。

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:41.750
<v Ian Underwood>It's like, if you wanted to design an instrument that could be easily broken by second and third graders, it would be the violin.
<v Ian Underwood>就像是，如果你想设计一种很容易被二三年级学生弄坏的乐器，那绝对是小提琴。

00:12:41.750 --> 00:13:10.130
<v Sarah Gibson>This is Ian Underwood. He's the guy who wrote that pamphlet. Ian's a member of the Free State Project, a movement of libertarians who, 20 years ago, started to move to New Hampshire with the goal of getting elected to local office and creating a barely-regulated, barely-taxed utopia of sorts. They're becoming a more serious political force with wins across the state. The majority leader in the House of Representatives, he moved here as a Free-Stater.
<v Sarah Gibson>这位是伊恩·安德伍德。他就是写那份小册子的人。伊恩是“自由州计划”的一员，这是一个自由意志主义者的运动，20年前开始移居新罕布什尔，目标是当选地方官员，创造一个几乎不受监管、几乎不征税的乌托邦。他们正成为一股更严肃的政治力量，在全州范围内取得胜利。众议院多数党领袖就是作为一名“自由州成员”搬到这里的。

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:28.550
<v Sarah Gibson>Ian has lived here for about 15 years on a dirt road above the Croydon schoolhouse. He thinks public school is a failure. One of the high schools down the road has really low test scores, and every year they ask for more money. So Ian, at the meeting, makes a proposal, something he'd been planning.
<v Sarah Gibson>伊恩已经在克罗伊登校舍上方的一条土路上住了大约15年。他认为公立学校是失败的。路尽头的一所高中考试成绩很差，而且每年都要求更多的钱。所以伊恩在会议上提出了一个提案，这是他一直计划好的。

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:48.890
<v Ian Underwood>I stood up and I said, what we're being presented with is a ransom, not a budget. I think, instead, the town should present them with a budget. You have a certain amount of money and you figure out how to make it work. And so that requires us to pick a number. Let's change it from $1.7 million to $800,000. I move that that be the budget number.
<v Ian Underwood>我站起来说，我们面前呈现的不是预算，而是勒索。我认为，相反，镇上应该提交一份预算。你有一笔钱，然后你想办法让它运作起来。所以这就要求我们选一个数字。让我们把它从170万美元改为80万美元。我动议将此作为预算金额。

00:13:48.890 --> 00:14:02.030
<v Sarah Gibson>Ian was proposing cutting the budget by more than half, basically blowing up the system. Jim, who in the past had been shot down proposing much smaller cuts, knew Ian was going to do this. And he was ready.
<v Sarah Gibson>伊恩提议将预算削减一半以上，基本上是要炸掉这个系统。过去曾因提议小额削减而被驳回的吉姆，知道伊恩会这么做。而且他准备好了。

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:14.030
<v Jim Peschke>When he made his motion, a bunch of us rushed to second it. A good four or five people jumped in to second it. There was an enthusiasm about, yes, let's do this. Let's have this discussion.
<v Jim Peschke>当他提出动议时，我们中的一群人冲上去附议。足足有四五个人跳出来附议。有一种热情，是的，让我们这么做吧。让我们讨论一下。

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:20.060
<v Sarah Gibson>Tom Moore, the school board member with the wooden pens who has three kids in school, started to panic.
<v Sarah Gibson>带着木笔、有三个孩子在学校上学的学校董事会成员汤姆·摩尔开始恐慌。

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:45.850
<v Tom Moore>And that's when I started really trying to speak to the audience. And I pointed out, look, if you cut this down to $800,000, this budget just does not work. And I'm looking around the room, and I'm trying to suss out, OK, how many people here are going to shut this down. And I'm not seeing too many of them. And I'm starting to realize, boy, this could go really sideways.
<v Tom Moore>那时我开始真正试图向观众喊话。我指出，看，如果你把它砍到80万美元，这个预算根本行不通。我环顾四周，试图摸清情况，好吧，这里有多少人会阻止它。但我没看到几个。我开始意识到，天哪，这可能会偏离轨道太远了。

00:14:45.850 --> 00:15:25.360
<v Sarah Gibson>Tom texts his wife, "Get down here, bring everyone you know." But the vote happens too fast. People raise their hands, yay or nay. The moderator counts. And the slashed budget passes, 20 to 14. The people of Croydon have spoken, the 4% of them who actually came to the meeting. Later, someone called a lawyer and a state official to see if there was any way to reverse this and learned the vote was binding.
<v Sarah Gibson>汤姆给妻子发短信：“快来这儿，带上你知道的所有人。”但投票进行得太快。人们举手，赞成或反对。主持人清点。削减后的预算以20比14通过了。克罗伊登的民众已经表态了，是那4%真正参加会议的人。后来，有人打电话给律师和州政府官员，看看有没有办法撤回这个决定，结果得知投票具有法律约束力。

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:33.580
<v Jennifer>He grabbed the bottle of whiskey, poured himself a glass, and he just said, you have no idea what just happened.
<v Jennifer>他拿起那瓶威士忌，给自己倒了一杯，说，你根本不知道刚刚发生了什么。

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:42.730
<v Sarah Gibson>This is Tom's wife, Jennifer. Tom's not much of a drinker, so she knew whatever happened was bad. And it was pretty bad for them.
<v Sarah Gibson>这是汤姆的妻子詹妮弗。汤姆不怎么喝酒，所以她知道发生了什么坏事。这对他们来说确实非常糟糕。

00:15:42.730 --> 00:16:07.180
<v Sarah Gibson>The new school budget meant that they, and many other parents, could have to start paying to keep their kids in public school. And it was a lot of money, potentially up to $9,000 per year, per kid. That's because Croydon is so tiny, it doesn't have its own middle and high school, so older kids go to school in nearby, bigger towns. And Croydon covers that tuition.
<v Sarah Gibson>新的学校预算意味着他们，以及许多其他家长，可能必须开始掏钱让孩子上公立学校。这是一大笔钱，每人每年可能高达9000美元。这是因为克罗伊登太小了，没有自己的初中和高中，所以大一点的孩子要去附近更大的城镇上学。克罗伊登负责这部分学费。

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:27.910
<v Sarah Gibson>But with the budget cut so drastically, the town could only afford to pay half the tuition at some of those schools. So parents would have to make up the difference. For Tom and Jennifer, sending their three kids to one of the medium-priced public schools, that'd be about $20,000 a year. That's money they don't have. A lot of families here don't.
<v Sarah Gibson>但由于预算被剧烈削减，小镇只能负担得起那些学校一半的学费。所以家长们必须自己补齐差额。对汤姆和詹妮弗来说，送三个孩子去中等价位的公立学校，那一年大约要两万美元。这笔钱他们没有。这里很多家庭也没有。

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:35.920
<v Jennifer>Like, we're going to have to look for another place to live, because we're not going to be able to afford sending our kids to school. I mean--
<v Jennifer>就像，我们得找别的地方住，因为我们将负担不起送孩子上学。我是说——

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:37.690
<v Tom Moore>And that's a conversation we never thought we would ever have.
<v Tom Moore>那是一个我们从未想过会有的对话。

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:38.800
<v Jennifer>We've never had that--
<v Jennifer>我们从没有过这种——

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:43.340
<v Sarah Gibson>For Tom, this cut felt like a violation of a basic tenet of our democracy.
<v Sarah Gibson>对汤姆来说，这次削减感觉是对我们民主基本原则的侵犯。

00:16:43.340 --> 00:17:04.730
<v Tom Moore>It baffles me. It just-- I don't understand it. I don't get it. I mean, we decided this centuries ago. I mean, we decided ages and ages ago, that this is what we should be doing as a country. We need to provide students with an adequate education through taxation.
<v Tom Moore>这让我困惑。它只是——我不明白。我不懂。我的意思是，我们几个世纪前就决定了。我的意思是，我们在很久很久以前就决定了，作为国家，这就是我们应该做的事。我们需要通过税收为学生提供足够的教育。

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:21.650
<v Sarah Gibson>There were some alternatives to paying to send your kid to public school. The school board said kids could enroll in a learning pod instead, managed by private companies. It'd be a mix of in-person and online learning. And it was cheap enough to fit within the slashed budget.
<v Sarah Gibson>有一些替代方案可以不用掏钱送孩子去公立学校。学校董事会说孩子们可以转而参加一个学习小组，由私人公司管理。那将是线下和线上学习的混合。而且它便宜到足以放入被削减的预算中。

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:52.720
<v Sarah Gibson>Tom says, this would be a crap show. His kids didn't do well with online learning during the pandemic. They felt distracted and isolated. Meanwhile, members of the Free State Project were going online and celebrating, saying, look at Croydon, this is how we slash taxes and transform public education. Croydon parents saw this kind of thing on Facebook and felt like, all of a sudden, their kids were part of some experiment. And they were pissed.
<v Sarah Gibson>汤姆说，这会是一场灾难。他的孩子在疫情期间的线上学习表现不好。他们感到分心和孤立。与此同时，“自由州计划”的成员们在网上庆祝，说，看看克罗伊登，这就是我们如何削减税收和改变公共教育的方法。克罗伊登的家长们在Facebook上看到了这类东西，觉得仿佛一夜之间，他们的孩子成了某种实验品。他们气坏了。

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:57.520
<v Woman>I'm gonna to call this meeting to order at 6:31.
<v Woman>我宣布会议于6点31分开始。

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.860
<v Sarah Gibson>Here's some tape from a school board meeting, post-budget cut. It's a mom talking about her son.
<v Sarah Gibson>这是一段预算削减后的学校董事会录音。是一位母亲在谈论她的儿子。

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:13.840
<v Amie Freak>And he's a senior next year. And if that's taken away from him after the last two years, I can't handle it. I can't do it. You cannot hurt the kids.
<v Amie Freak>他明年就是高三了。如果在那过去的两年之后，他又被剥夺了这一切，我承受不住。我做不到。你们不能伤害孩子们。

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:17.950
<v Amie Freak>[APPLAUSE]
<v Amie Freak>[掌声]

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:48.370
<v Sarah Gibson>That meeting, significantly better attended. These parents had been told the budget cut was binding. But they thought, there must be some way out of this. They started calling around, going down internet rabbit holes, and looking for an escape route because democracies are never as simple as just a bunch of people voting. There's always a clause somewhere-- a guardrail for Americans against themselves. And then-- and it's not exactly clear who found it. A lot of people say it was this woman Lorraine.
<v Sarah Gibson>那次会议的出席人数明显多了很多。家长们被告知预算削减具有约束力。但他们想，一定有办法摆脱这个困境。他们开始四处打电话，在互联网的深洞里寻找，寻找逃生路线，因为民主永远不会像一群人投票那么简单。总是会有某个条款——作为美国人防止自我伤害的护栏。然后——而且并不完全清楚是谁发现的。很多人说是这个叫洛林的女士。

00:18:48.370 --> 00:19:23.830
<v Sarah Gibson>But in any case, parents land on something buried deep in New Hampshire state law. RSA 197:2-- it basically says that they can petition to hold a special meeting where the town gets another chance to vote on the school budget. But it's a long shot. Not only would parents have to win the revote. The revote would only count if half of croydon's registered voters show up and cast a ballot. That's 283 people. For Tom, who used to be on the Croydon School Board, that number seemed impossible.
<v Sarah Gibson>但无论如何，家长们发现了一些埋藏在新罕布什尔州法律深处的东西。RSA 197:2——它基本上说，他们可以请愿举行一次特别会议，让城镇有另一次机会投票决定学校预算。但这是一个难点。家长们不仅要赢得重新投票。重新投票只有在克罗伊登一半的注册选民出席并投票的情况下才算数。那是283人。对于曾经在学校董事会任职的汤姆来说，那个数字看起来不可能实现。

00:19:23.830 --> 00:19:32.470
<v Tom Moore>It's a big number. It's a very small town. We've never, ever, ever had that many people show up to vote on something.
<v Tom Moore>这是一个很大的数字。这是一个非常小的城镇。我们从未，从未，从未有那么多人出来投票表决什么事。

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:58.390
<v Sarah Gibson>He says the school board was lucky if five or six people came to watch. After I heard about this revote, I decided to stick around Croydon because I really could not tell what would happen, and because this seemed like a test for some big questions a lot of Americans are asking about the future of public schools and our democracy.
<v Sarah Gibson>他说学校董事会运气好也就五六个人来旁听。在听说这个重新投票后，我决定留在克罗伊登，因为我真的看不出会发生什么，而且因为这似乎是一场测试，测试很多美国人正在提出的关于公立学校和我们民主未来的重大问题。

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:33.330
<v Sarah Gibson>Slashing the school budget to $800,000 would save each household in Croydon an average of $2,000 a year. And this town leans conservative, went for Trump. A lot of people, they want lower taxes and minimal government. And the question of the school budget was not going to be decided with PAC money or TV ads, but the old fashioned way-- neighbors talking to neighbors-- people they actually know-- to try to persuade them, one by one.
<v Sarah Gibson>将学校预算削减到80万美元，每个克罗伊登家庭平均每年可以节省2000美元。这个城镇倾向于保守，投票支持了特朗普。很多人想要更低的税收和最小化的政府。关于学校预算的问题不会由政治行动委员会的资金或电视广告来决定，而是以老派的方式——邻居对邻居交谈——那些他们真正认识的人——试图一个接一个地说服他们。

00:20:33.330 --> 00:21:04.590
<v Sarah Gibson>They needed 283 voters to show up in person. And they had a tight deadline, about 40 days. One thing about democracy the founding fathers forgot to tell us-- it can be a real pain in the ass. To reverse the budget cut, a group of mostly public school parents put their lives on hold. The first three weeks were an organizing frenzy-- meetings, constant Facebook messaging. They ordered lawn signs and hand-painted bigger ones that they stuck in the back of pickup trucks and put all over town.
<v Sarah Gibson>他们需要283名选民亲自出席。他们有一个紧迫的截止日期，大约40天。关于民主，开国元勋们忘了告诉我们一件事——它真的可以是个非常麻烦的事情。为了撤销预算削减，一群大多是公立学校家长的组织把生活暂停了。前三周是组织上的狂热——会议、不断的Facebook信息。他们订购了草坪标志，手工绘制了更大的，插在皮卡车后面，贴遍全镇。

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:31.170
<v Sarah Gibson>There were two moms at the center of this, sisters-- twin sisters, actually-- Angi and Amie, who I spent a lot of time with. Amie is the one you heard in that town meeting talking about her high school son. A month and a half after the meeting in March, with just one week to go before the revote, Amie and Angi are driving from house to house, trying to convince people to come to the revote. They've never done a campaign like this before.
<v Sarah Gibson>在这项运动的核心有两位妈妈，姐妹——其实是双胞胎姐妹——安吉和艾米，我和她们待了很长时间。艾米就是你在那场镇会议上听到谈论她高中儿子的那个人。三月份会议过后一个半月，距离重新投票还有一周，艾米和安吉开车挨家挨户拜访，试图说服人们参加重新投票。她们以前从未做过这样的竞选活动。

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:38.610
<v Angi Beaulieu>We're bad at this. They say, we're not registered voters. OK, that's fine.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我们不擅长这个。他们说，我们不是注册选民。好的，没关系。

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:55.680
<v Sarah Gibson>Angi and Amie grew up in Croydon. Their car has an American flag tinted on the back window. They've been well-liked since they were kids. In high school, Amie got voted best all-around. Angi-- most sarcastic. The Freaks, they're called. That's their family's last name.
<v Sarah Gibson>安吉和艾米在克罗伊登长大。她们的车后窗上贴着一面美国国旗。她们从小就讨人喜欢。在高中，艾米被评为“最佳全能学生”。安吉——“最爱讽刺人”。她们被称为“怪胎”。那是她们家族的姓氏。

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:03.160
<v Amie Freak>I kept my name. I married. My wife Cally took my name-- Freak. So we're Freaks. And everybody knows us, and they love it.
<v Amie Freak>我保留了我的姓。我结婚了。我的妻子凯莉用了我的姓——怪胎。所以我们是怪胎。大家都认识我们，也喜欢这个。

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.040
<v Angi Beaulieu>Everybody knows us.
<v Angi Beaulieu>大家都认识我们。

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.550
<v Sarah Gibson>They go to visit a guy named Brian, whose mom is there.
<v Sarah Gibson>她们去拜访一个叫布莱恩的人，他的妈妈也在那儿。

