<v Angi Beaulieu>We are out chatting with people about coming to the meeting on Saturday. And I know that you guys are up in the air about it.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我们正在四处聊天，关于周六去参加会议的事。我知道你们对此还在犹豫。

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:53.100
<v Nick Avery>I'm not up in the air about it. I'm not going.
<v Nick Avery>我没犹豫。我不打算去。

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:54.410
<v Angi Beaulieu>You're not going.
<v Angi Beaulieu>你不去。

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:54.810
<v Nick Avery>Nope.
<v Nick Avery>不去。

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.230
<v Angi Beaulieu>OK. Can you tell me why?
<v Angi Beaulieu>好的。能告诉我为什么吗？

00:22:58.230 --> 00:22:59.370
<v Nick Avery>It's just my choice.
<v Nick Avery>这只是我的选择。

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.990
<v Angi Beaulieu>OK. OK.
<v Angi Beaulieu>好的。好的。

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:02.940
<v Nick Avery>I don't want any hard feelings in this little town.
<v Nick Avery>我不想在这个小镇上有什么隔阂。

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:05.100
<v Angi Beaulieu>Yeah. OK.
<v Angi Beaulieu>是的。好的。

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:08.090
<v Amie Freak>Do you think-- the vote's going to make hard feelings? I still love you.
<v Amie Freak>你觉得——投票会导致隔阂吗？我依然爱你。

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:11.880
<v Nick Avery>No, I think me not going could.
<v Nick Avery>不，我觉得我不去可能会。

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:22.890
<v Sarah Gibson>Before they went out door knocking today, Angi and Amie got advice from a professional campaign organizer that if someone says no, just move on. Reasonable advice-- they ignore it.
<v Sarah Gibson>今天出门敲门之前，安吉和艾米从一位专业竞选组织者那里得到了建议，如果有人拒绝，就继续前进。合理的建议——她们忽略了。

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:24.810
<v Nick Avery>I've lived here my whole life. So have you guys.
<v Nick Avery>我在这里住了一辈子。你们也是。

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:25.800
<v Angi Beaulieu>Yeah, absolutely.
<v Angi Beaulieu>是的，绝对。

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:31.070
<v Amie Freak>It's honestly a little disappointing, just because I'm going to have to pay for Caleb for his senior year, you know?
<v Amie Freak>说实话这有点让人失望，仅仅是因为我得为凯莱布的高中最后一年买单，你知道吗？

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:32.850
<v Nick Avery>I get it. I get it.
<v Nick Avery>我明白。我明白。

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:34.260
<v Amie Freak>I don't think that's fair.
<v Amie Freak>我不觉得这公平。

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:35.790
<v Nick Avery>I'm not saying it's fair.
<v Nick Avery>我没说这公平。

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:41.430
<v Amie Freak>Do you think, because we didn't go vote, we weren't there at the meeting? That that's why you're--
<v Amie Freak>你认为，是因为我们没去投票，我们没去参加会议？这就是为什么你——

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:42.510
<v Angi Beaulieu>Oh, yeah, she didn't go.
<v Angi Beaulieu>噢，是的，她没去。

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:49.320
<v Amie Freak>I didn't go to the meeting. I got my hand slapped. I will never miss another meeting. I'll tell you what.
<v Amie Freak>我没去参加会议。我受教训了。我再也不会错过任何会议了。我告诉你吧。

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:54.090
<v Nick Avery>I'll be honest with you. $1.7 million is way too much for this town.
<v Nick Avery>我实话实说。170万美元对这个镇来说太多了。

00:23:54.090 --> 00:24:05.340
<v Sarah Gibson>Nick starts talking about Croydon's one-room schoolhouse, still in operation, which he and the sisters went to as kids. He doesn't get why it needs so many more staff and resources these days.
<v Sarah Gibson>尼克开始谈论克罗伊登的一间教室校舍，至今仍在运作，他和这对姐妹小时候都去过。他不明白为什么现在需要这么多额外的工作人员和资源。

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:14.790
<v Nick Avery>You know, when we were there, we had two rows for first grade, two rows for second grade, and two rows for third grade. We had a teacher that assigned stuff, and we had--
<v Nick Avery>你知道，当我们在那儿的时候，一年级两排，二年级两排，三年级两排。我们有一位老师分配作业，我们有——

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:15.330
<v Amie Freak>An aide.
<v Amie Freak>一名助手。

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:17.820
<v Nick Avery>--and aide or two aides to help out with the kids.
<v Nick Avery>——一名助手或两名助手来帮忙照看孩子。

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:22.500
<v Amie Freak>Yeah, I remember that. We absolutely did. Now, can I--
<v Amie Freak>是的，我记得。我们绝对有。现在，我可以——

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:23.460
<v Nick Avery>You can say anything you want.
<v Nick Avery>你想说什么都行。

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:29.280
<v Amie Freak>I want to ask you a question. So $1.7 is too much. We're operating at $1.5 right now, right?
<v Amie Freak>我想问你一个问题。所以170万太多了。我们现在以150万运作，对吧？

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:29.670
<v Angi Beaulieu>Yep.
<v Angi Beaulieu>是的。

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:33.660
<v Amie Freak>They cannot operate what they're trying to do with $800,000.
<v Amie Freak>他们没法用80万美元运作他们想做的事。

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:43.980
<v Nick Avery>We all know that the state of New Hampshire is not going to allow kids not to go to school. Do you honestly think that, come fall, you're going to have to pay nine grand out of your pocket to send your kid to school?
<v Nick Avery>我们都知道新罕布什尔州不会允许孩子不上学。你真的认为，到了秋天，你得自掏腰包掏9000块送你孩子上学吗？

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:44.850
<v Amie Freak>Yes.
<v Amie Freak>是的。

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:53.040
<v Nick Avery>But it's a public school. They're not going to turn the kid away. They're not going to get to the school, and the principal's going to come out and go, sorry, little fellow, you got to go home because you're a Croydon kid.
<v Nick Avery>但这可是公立学校。他们不会拒绝孩子入学。孩子到了学校，校长不会出来说，抱歉，小伙子，你得回家，因为你是克罗伊登的孩子。

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:59.250
<v Angi Beaulieu>I'm pretty sure that if we don't pay, that's what they're going to do. Do you want me to find that out for you? Do you want me to call Newport?
<v Angi Beaulieu>我相当确定如果我们不交钱，他们就会那么做。你想让我为你去查查吗？你想让我给纽波特打个电话吗？

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:03.650
<v Nick Avery>I just-- I can't for the life of me believe--
<v Nick Avery>我只是——我无论如何都不敢相信——

00:25:03.650 --> 00:25:17.080
<v Angi Beaulieu>This is what I'm going to do. Just so we can continue this conversation, I'm going to call the Department of Revenue tomorrow and speak with a representative there. And I meant to ask them this this past week, actually. So I'm glad that you brought this up.
<v Angi Beaulieu>这就是我打算做的。为了继续我们的对话，我明天要给税务局打电话，跟那里的代表谈谈。其实上周我就想问他们这个问题。所以我很高兴你提到了这一点。

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:22.310
<v Nick Avery>But the other problem is, people around here have watched that budget go up and up and up and up.
<v Nick Avery>但另一个问题是，这里的人们看着那个预算涨啊涨啊，一直涨。

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:22.560
<v Angi Beaulieu>Right.
<v Angi Beaulieu>没错。

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:27.390
<v Nick Avery>And nobody's really said much till now. And now everybody's crying wolf.
<v Nick Avery>直到现在都没人真正说什么。现在大家都在喊“狼来了”。

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:42.590
<v Angi Beaulieu>Listen, if they had cut it by $200,000, this would not be happening. Honestly, a $200,000 cut would not change-- there's a turkey right there. [LAUGHS] Did you go out this morning?
<v Angi Beaulieu>听着，如果他们砍掉20万美元，这事儿就不会发生了。说实话，砍掉20万美元不会改变——那里有一只火鸡。[笑] 你今天早上出去了吗？

00:25:42.590 --> 00:26:04.280
<v Sarah Gibson>May in New Hampshire is wild turkey hunting season. By the end, the sisters have spent 30 minutes with Nick. It doesn't move them any closer to their magic number of 283 voters. But it's something you don't see much these days-- people who don't agree actually talking to each other and listening. And it was nice to hear.
<v Sarah Gibson>五月在新罕布什尔是野生火鸡狩猎季。最后，姐妹俩和尼克聊了30分钟。这并没有让她们更接近283名选民的魔法数字。但这是一种你现在很少见到的场景——意见不同的人确实在交谈并倾听对方。这听起来很不错。

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:07.090
<v Angi Beaulieu>Thank you, Nick. Sorry to interrupt your work.
<v Angi Beaulieu>谢谢你，尼克。抱歉打断你的工作。

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:09.170
<v Nick Avery>It's all right. Next time, I'll put you to work.
<v Nick Avery>没事。下次，我让你帮我干活。

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:10.430
<v Angi Beaulieu>She said!
<v Angi Beaulieu>她说得对！

00:26:10.430 --> 00:26:39.140
<v Sarah Gibson>By the way, Angi and other parents did talk to neighboring school districts about whether they'd let in Croydon students for half price. And they were basically told, no way. Meanwhile, the other side has a way easier campaign ahead of them because the only thing the budget cutters need people to do is nothing-- just skip the meeting.
<v Sarah Gibson>顺便说一句，安吉和其他家长确实和邻近的学区谈过是否愿意半价接收克罗伊登的学生。基本上都被告知没门。与此同时，另一方有一场轻松得多的竞选，因为预算削减者只需要人们做一件事，就是什么都不做——只要别去参加会议就行。

00:26:39.140 --> 00:27:02.630
<v Sarah Gibson>That way, the revote won't meet quorum, and it won't count. To reinforce this message, the man who proposed the budget cut back in March sends out mailers. They say, "If you like the budget you have, you can keep it. Just stay home on May 7." Jim Paschke, the budget nerd who'd been trying to cut the budget for years, is feeling pretty confident.
<v Sarah Gibson>这样，重新投票就不会达到法定人数，也就无效了。为了加强这一信息，三月份提议削减预算的人发出了邮件。他们说：“如果你喜欢你现在的预算，你可以保留它。5月7日呆在家里就行。”一直试图削减预算的预算书呆子吉姆·佩什克感到非常自信。

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:20.750
<v Jim Peschke>I think there's a false narrative that a bunch of kooks voted for this budget, and the whole town is against it. I don't think that's true. And the problem with getting people to go is there's only a handful of people in Croydon who truly benefit from the higher budget.
<v Jim Peschke>我认为存在一种虚假的叙事，说一群疯子投票支持这个预算，而整个镇都反对它。我不认为这是真的。而且说服人们参加的问题在于，克罗伊登只有少数几个人真正从高预算中受益。

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:32.430
<v Sarah Gibson>The way he sees the math, there are only 80 or so kids in the school district. So sure, their families will show up to restore the budget. But he doesn't think they'll get enough of their neighbors to join them.
<v Sarah Gibson>按照他的数学逻辑，学区里只有大约80个孩子。所以当然，他们的家庭会出来恢复预算。但他认为他们无法说服足够的邻居加入他们。

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:38.570
<v Amanda Leslie>--the numbers and the updated calling list. Tomorrow, I know we've got the--
<v Amanda Leslie>——数字和更新的电话名单。明天，我知道我们有——

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:54.180
<v Sarah Gibson>It's Wednesday, May 4, three days till the revote. Some of the parents organizing, who call themselves We Stand Up For Croydon, gather in private-- start tallying up the confirmed numbers. They want to know how close they are to 283.
<v Sarah Gibson>5月4日星期三，距离重新投票还有三天。一些组织活动的家长，自称“我们为克罗伊登站出来”，私下聚集在一起——开始汇总确认的数字。他们想知道离283人还差多少。

00:27:54.180 --> 00:28:00.470
<v Amanda Leslie>Zandra, do you want to talk a little bit about our updated list?
<v Amanda Leslie>桑德拉，你想谈谈我们更新的名单吗？

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:09.590
<v Zandra>We look at 275 supporters ID'ed so far-- 236 who can attend. So that's a big drop off.
<v Zandra>我们目前确认了275名支持者——236人可以参加。所以有很大的流失。

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:11.330
<v Angi Beaulieu>That's terrible.
<v Angi Beaulieu>太糟糕了。

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:20.210
<v Sarah Gibson>That's Angi saying it's terrible. They need about 50 more. The next day is the final push-- the phone bank.
<v Sarah Gibson>那是安吉在说太糟糕了。他们还需要50人左右。第二天是最后的冲刺——电话拉票。

00:28:20.210 --> 00:28:27.980
<v Angi Beaulieu>Can we call Brenda and Roderick and just confirm? I have called, texted, and Facebook messaged Brad with no response.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我们可以打给布伦达和罗德里克确认一下吗？我给布拉德打过电话、发过短信和Facebook消息，但没回复。

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:49.220
<v Sarah Gibson>Angi's here in her go-to outfit-- t-shirt, jeans, eyeliner, and mascara. There's not good cell phone service in Croydon. So the volunteers have gone to a nearby town, ordered pizza. They have a big list of names, and they start making calls. Angi's focused. She rubs her temples a lot. There's a problem with the list of names.
<v Sarah Gibson>安吉穿着她标志性的服装——T恤、牛仔裤、眼线和睫毛膏。克罗伊登的手机信号不好。所以志愿者们去了附近的一个城镇，点了披萨。他们有一长串名字，开始打电话。安吉很专注。她经常揉太阳穴。名单有问题。

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:53.450
<v Angi Beaulieu>Well, the tricky thing is we don't know if there's duplicates.
<v Angi Beaulieu>嗯，棘手的是我们不知道有没有重复的人。

00:28:53.450 --> 00:29:08.840
<v Sarah Gibson>The majority of volunteers here are-- surprise, surprise-- moms. Most have jobs and kids who are currently in school. They've disagreed on some things in the past like mask mandates. But when it comes to funding public schools, they're all on the same page.
<v Sarah Gibson>这里的志愿者大多是——出人意料——妈妈们。大多数人有工作，孩子正在上学。过去她们在一些事情上有分歧，比如口罩强制令。但当涉及到公立学校经费时，她们都在同一阵线上。

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:10.490
<v Amanda Leslie>Is there any way I might--
<v Amanda Leslie>有没有什么办法我可能——

00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:26.720
<v Sarah Gibson>One of them is Amanda Leslie, mom of two, English teacher, who also grew up here. Usually when she and I talk about the budget cut, she uses a lot of colorful language. But when she gets on the phone, she puts on her teacher hat. She calls a family she's known for years.
<v Sarah Gibson>其中之一是阿曼达·莱斯利，两个孩子的母亲，英语老师，也是在这儿长大的。通常我和她讨论预算削减时，她会用很多激烈的语言。但当她打电话时，她戴上了教师的帽子。她给一个认识多年的家庭打了电话。

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:59.060
<v Amanda Leslie>Can I tell you a little bit of new information? No? OK. Would Ed be willing to talk with me since he will be there? Super-- that'd be great. Hey, Ed, how are you doing? Good. I understand you're going to help out at the meeting on Saturday as a ballot clerk. Can I ask, are you going to also vote while you're there? You're not going to vote? Oh, I'm so disappointed.
<v Amanda Leslie>能告诉你一点新信息吗？不行？好的。那艾德愿意和我谈谈吗，因为他那天会在那儿？太好了——那太棒了。嘿，艾德，你好吗？好的。我知道周六你会作为投票员在会上帮忙。我可以问一下，你在那儿的时候会投票吗？你不打算投票？噢，我太失望了。

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:34.690
<v Amanda Leslie>Right, but even though it's a legal thing-- I hear you. I absolutely hear that. You know, I was there too. It certainly was legal. But there is this opportunity within the democratic process to revisit it. And, I mean, do you think you could tell me that you'll think about it? OK. Well, again, I'm disappointed. But thank you for talking with me, Ed. All right, I'll see you Saturday.
<v Amanda Leslie>没错，尽管这是合法的——我听到了。我完全听到了。你知道，我当时也在那儿。那当然合法。但民主进程中确实有这样一个重新审视的机会。而且，我是说，你觉得你能告诉我你会考虑一下吗？好的。好吧，再一次，我很失望。但谢谢你跟我谈话，艾德。好的，周六见。

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:38.480
<v Amanda Leslie>Ugh. Break my fricking heart.
<v Amanda Leslie>呃。简直伤透了我的心。

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:43.070
<v Sarah Gibson>My producer, Chris, is with Amanda when she ends the call with Ed.
<v Sarah Gibson>我的制片人克里斯在阿曼达结束与艾德的通话时和她在一起。

00:30:43.070 --> 00:30:46.370
<v Chris Benderev>Is it easier or harder when you know them?
<v Chris Benderev>当你认识他们时，这更容易还是更难？

00:30:46.370 --> 00:31:10.700
<v Amanda Leslie>I think it's more heartbreaking, you know? These people that I've known the entire time I've lived in Croydon-- and my in-laws-- they've known them for 40, 50, 60 years, you know? And it's just really sad, especially because I know that they're good people. They're not total a-holes, you know?
<v Amanda Leslie>我觉得更心碎，你知道吗？这些人我住在这个镇上一直都认识——还有我的姻亲——他们认识他们40、50、60年了，你知道吗？这真的很悲伤，尤其是因为我知道他们是好人。他们不是混蛋，你知道吗？

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:35.900
<v Sarah Gibson>The guy Amanda was on the phone with-- Ed-- he had concerns I've heard from other people in town. They don't think the May 7th revote is fair. The town already made its decision in March. If people wanted to be part of the budget process and ensure their kids' school got funded, why didn't they show up then?
<v Sarah Gibson>阿曼达通话的那个人——艾德——他有我从镇上其他人那里听到过的担忧。他们认为5月7日的重新投票不公平。小镇三月份已经做出了决定。如果人们想参与预算进程并确保他们孩子的学校得到资助，为什么那时候不去？

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:58.640
<v Sarah Gibson>May 6th, the evening before the revote. The parents just finished setting up the space-- a YMCA camp, which is the biggest venue in the area they could find. The Croydon town hall couldn't fit 283 people. Angi is in the parking lot, headed to her SUV. She has a new concern. She just heard that her opponents want to make a presentation before the vote.
<v Sarah Gibson>5月6日，重新投票的前一天晚上。家长们刚布置完场地——一个YMCA营地，这是他们能找到的附近最大的场地。克罗伊登市政厅装不下283人。安吉在停车场，走向她的SUV。她有一个新的担忧。她刚听说对手想在投票前做一个演讲。

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.760
<v Sarah Gibson>Wait, so when did you guys find out that Aaron is giving a presentation?
<v Sarah Gibson>等一下，你们什么时候发现亚伦要进行演讲的？

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:08.460
<v Angi Beaulieu>Today, tonight, here-- just found out. The moderator is who let us know.
<v Angi Beaulieu>今天，今晚，在这儿——刚发现的。是主持人告诉我们的。

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:16.710
<v Sarah Gibson>This presentation is totally allowed. But Angi wonders if it's a tactic to run the clock so people will leave before the vote.
<v Sarah Gibson>这个演讲是完全允许的。但安吉怀疑这是一种拖延时间的策略，这样人们在投票前就会离开。

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:20.940
<v Sarah Gibson>Because at what point are you worried that people are going to start leaving?
<v Sarah Gibson>因为在什么时间点你担心人们会开始离开？

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:43.500
<v Angi Beaulieu>Probably an hour or an hour and a half in, I think that people will start losing interest. And, I mean, they have other things to do-- legit other things to do. We have people that have funerals in Scutney, Vermont. We have people that have weddings. We have people that have college graduation in Keene. I mean, we can't ask them to sit here for hours and hours.
<v Angi Beaulieu>可能一小时或一小时半之后，我觉得人们会开始失去兴趣。而且，我是说，他们还有别的事要做——确实还有别的事要做。我们有人要在佛蒙特州的斯卡特尼参加葬礼。我们有人要参加婚礼。我们有人要在基恩参加大学毕业典礼。我的意思是，我们不能要求他们在这里坐上几个小时又几个小时。

00:32:43.500 --> 00:33:03.930
<v Sarah Gibson>Angi's also a bit frustrated because some people don't have plans but are like, I don't know if I can find two hours to spare to come. And Angie feels she and other moms have been working nonstop to save Croydon's public school system. Angi's accumulated stress-- it reached a breaking point when she and some volunteers were setting up today.
<v Sarah Gibson>安吉也有些沮丧，因为有些人没什么计划，却说：“我不知道能不能抽出两小时来。”而安吉觉得她和其他妈妈们一直在不间断地努力拯救克罗伊登的公立学校系统。安吉积累的压力——当她和一些志愿者今天布置场地时，达到了崩溃点。

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:09.140
<v Angi Beaulieu>And so I had a minor meltdown.
<v Angi Beaulieu>所以我也经历了一次轻微的崩溃。

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:10.700
<v Chris Benderev>What did that look like?
<v Chris Benderev>那看起来是什么样的？

00:33:10.700 --> 00:33:18.020
<v Angi Beaulieu>No tears. Some frustration-- enough so that people thought I needed a hug.
<v Angi Beaulieu>没有眼泪。有些挫败感——足以让人们认为我需要一个拥抱。

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.360
<v Sarah Gibson>Wait, what did you say?
<v Sarah Gibson>等一下，你说了什么？

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:28.430
<v Angi Beaulieu>Well, my sister said, what do you need from us right now? I said, I need everyone to shut the fuck up. That's what I said, if you want to be frank about it.
<v Angi Beaulieu>嗯，我妹妹说，你现在需要我们做什么？我说，我需要每个人都闭上臭嘴。这就是我说的，如果你想坦诚点的话。

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:45.710
<v Sarah Gibson>Angi's not sleeping great. I'll send her a text in the middle of the day-- get a response at 3:00 AM the next morning. Angi tells me she's still feeling optimistic. But no one knows how tomorrow will play out. Here's Jennifer, Tom's wife. She tells me she's not sure it's going to work.
<v Sarah Gibson>安吉睡得不太好。我会在白天给她发短信——第二天凌晨3点才收到回复。安吉告诉我她依然感到乐观。但没人知道明天会怎样。这是詹妮弗，汤姆的妻子。她告诉我她不确定这是否会奏效。

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:57.770
<v Jennifer>I think it's going to be so close. I think it's going to be within a vote or two. And that's why we so desperately are reaching out to anyone we can. And it could go either way, honestly.
<v Jennifer>我觉得会非常接近。我觉得会只差一两票。这就是为什么我们拼命地联系我们能联系到的任何人。老实说，情况可能向任何一方倾斜。

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:03.290
<v Sarah Gibson>Her husband Tom feels the same. The other side seems much more relaxed.
<v Sarah Gibson>她的丈夫汤姆感觉也一样。另一方似乎轻松得多。

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:04.940
<v Jim Peschke>I'm going turkey hunting.
<v Jim Peschke>我要去打猎火鸡。

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:13.010
<v Sarah Gibson>This is Jim Peschke, talking about what he's going to do tomorrow during the revote. He's come up with a metaphor. I've heard it before at a school board meeting.
<v Sarah Gibson>这是吉姆·佩什克，谈论他明天重新投票期间要做的事。他想出了一个隐喻。我以前在学校董事会会议上听过。

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:33.440
<v Jim Peschke>And if you're not familiar, I'll be sitting there with a call, trying to trick the turkeys into coming towards me under the promise of finding a hen. And I can only speak from theory because I have yet to take one. I'm a lousy turkey hunter. But I think we're going to both be coming home empty-handed on the 7th.
<v Jim Peschke>如果你不熟悉，我会坐在那儿用叫声，试图诱骗火鸡朝我走来，承诺能找到一只母火鸡。而且我只能从理论上说，因为我还没抓到一只。我是个蹩脚的火鸡猎人。但我认为我们俩在7号都会空手而归。

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:56.870
<v Sarah Gibson>Get it? He won't get a turkey, and the parents won't get their big budget back. Finally, the day of the vote-- May 7. Voters are lined up at the registration table by 8:00 AM. It's cold. There's no heating in the hall, but there's a fireplace roaring at one end.
<v Sarah Gibson>明白了吗？他抓不到火鸡，家长们也找不回他们的大预算。终于，投票日到了——5月7日。选民们在早上8点前就在登记台排起了长队。天气很冷。大厅里没有暖气，但一端有一堆熊熊燃烧的壁炉。

00:34:56.870 --> 00:35:13.880
<v Sarah Gibson>For every democratic process, there are rules and often a public official responsible for enforcing them. Here, it's the moderator-- a man named Bruce Jasper. Today is his first time moderating a town meeting. And if Angi were hoping this would go quickly, it's not looking good.
<v Sarah Gibson>对于每一个民主进程，都有规则，通常还有负责执行规则的公职人员。这里，是主持人——一个叫布鲁斯·贾斯珀的人。今天是第一次主持镇会议。如果安吉希望这能快点进行，情况看起来不妙。

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:28.460
<v Bruce Jasper>So, just a little bit about me-- I was born in Nashua, New Hampshire. I'm the oldest of four children brought up on a poultry farm. My first job was a paper route delivering, the Nashua Telegraph on my bicycle.
<v Bruce Jasper>所以，讲讲我自己——我出生在新罕布什尔州的纳舒厄。我是四个在养鸡场长大的孩子中的老大。我的第一份工作是在自行车上送《纳舒厄电讯报》。

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:40.400
<v Sarah Gibson>To be clear, he's not trying to run down the clock. He just seems really into being the moderator. He gives the number of chickens on his childhood farm, the number of people in his graduating high school class.
<v Sarah Gibson>说明一下，他不是想故意拖延时间。他只是看起来真的很享受做主持人。他讲了小时候农场里的鸡的数量，他高中毕业班的人数。

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:57.380
<v Bruce Jasper>Currently, I'm a member of the Newport Rotary Club, where I've been secretary for the last 38 years and also on the board of Wags and Wiggles, a dog rescue organization in Newport.
<v Bruce Jasper>目前，我是纽波特扶轮社的成员，过去38年里一直担任秘书，也是纽波特一家叫“摇尾巴”的狗救援组织的董事会成员。

00:35:57.380 --> 00:36:19.480
<v Sarah Gibson>At first, people figure, maybe Bruce is stalling to give stragglers time to find their seats. But pretty soon, it's 9:20 AM. And people are getting antsy-- glancing at each other. Wait, this is the school budget revote, right? Bruce eventually transitions from his biography to the rules for the proceedings. He loves a good rule.
<v Sarah Gibson>起初，人们觉得，也许布鲁斯在拖延给迟到者时间找座位。但很快，到了上午9点20分。人们开始不耐烦了——互相斜视。等等，这是学校预算重新投票，对吧？布鲁斯最终从他的传记过渡到了会议规则。他喜欢好规则。

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:35.630
<v Bruce Jasper>The articles to be considered-- yep, I am. I am reading all of the rules so that everybody-- I don't know. I mean, if everybody has a copy of the rules and everybody is-- OK, I will stop here.
<v Bruce Jasper>要审议的条款——是的，我是。我在宣读所有的规则，以便每个人——我不知道。我是说，如果每个人都有一份规则副本，每个人都——好的，我在这里停下。

00:36:35.630 --> 00:36:42.050
<v Sarah Gibson>Someone eventually shouts out, move on! Angi and Tom give their presentation, and then--
<v Sarah Gibson>有人最终喊道，继续！安吉和汤姆做了他们的演讲，然后——

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:50.880
<v Angi Beaulieu>We have two student speakers that have very brief statements that they would like to make if we can have them come up and as long as no one opposes.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我们有两位学生发言人，如果没人反对，且我们能让他们上台，他们有非常简短的声明想说。

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:52.220
<v Bruce Jasper>OK. And again, I'm--
<v Bruce Jasper>好的。再说明一下，我——

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:54.660
<v Bruce Jasper>[APPLAUSE]
<v Bruce Jasper>[掌声]

00:36:54.660 --> 00:37:03.410
<v Bruce Jasper>The request is supposed to come through the moderator, all right? Again, it's in the rules. And I know everybody said they read it, and they all know them.
<v Bruce Jasper>要求应该通过主持人提出，好吗？再说一次，规则里有。我知道每个人都说他们读过了，他们都知道。

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:11.090
<v Angi Beaulieu>Tell me what you need me to do, Bruce. I am asking you to please allow the student speakers to be a part of our presentation.
<v Angi Beaulieu>告诉我你需要我怎么做，布鲁斯。我请求你允许学生发言人作为我们演讲的一部分。

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:16.370
<v Bruce Jasper>The procedure would be to ask the moderator to allow somebody to speak.
<v Bruce Jasper>程序应该是请求主持人允许某人发言。

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:19.370
<v Man>Will you allow them to speak?
<v Man>你允许他们发言吗？

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:24.920
<v Bruce Jasper>So, Angi, yes, if you're asking me, can we allow the--
<v Bruce Jasper>所以，安吉，是的，如果你问我，我们能允许——

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:29.300
<v Angi Beaulieu>Bruce, may I please have a student speaker speak right now during our presentation?
<v Angi Beaulieu>布鲁斯，请允许学生发言人现在在我们的演讲中发言好吗？

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:34.910
<v Bruce Jasper>And the answer is, yes, if it's informational. So my ruling is yes.
<v Bruce Jasper>答案是，是的，如果那是为了提供信息。所以我的裁定是肯定的。

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:36.470
<v Angi Beaulieu>Thank you.
<v Angi Beaulieu>谢谢。

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:54.680
<v Sarah Gibson>The two high schoolers, one after another, deliver their speeches. Parents had this plan a while ago. Let the kids have their say. It's about their future. But sticking to it is kind of surprising, given how anxious they are about time. I find Angi's sister, Amie, near the breakfast buffet.
<v Sarah Gibson>两名高中生，一个接一个地发表了演讲。家长们很早之前就有这个计划。让孩子们有发言权。这是关于他们的未来。但坚持这个计划有点令人惊讶，考虑到他们对时间的焦虑。我在早餐自助餐附近找到了安吉的妹妹艾米。

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:55.670
<v Student>I'm preparing to take--
<v Student>我准备选修——

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:57.100
<v Sarah Gibson>Is this going how you had hoped?
<v Sarah Gibson>进展和你希望的一样吗？

00:37:57.100 --> 00:38:01.100
<v Student>--college classes along with advanced literature and pre-calculus.
<v Student>——大学课程以及高级文学和微积分预科。

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:06.230
<v Amie Freak>Not really. I already wanted to be in line to vote.
<v Amie Freak>并不是。我早就想去排队投票了。

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:07.130
<v Sarah Gibson>What time is it right now?
<v Sarah Gibson>现在几点了？

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:10.150
<v Amie Freak>9:35. 9:35.
<v Amie Freak>9点35分。9点35分。

00:38:10.150 --> 00:38:11.800
<v Sarah Gibson>When are people going to start leaving?
<v Sarah Gibson>人们什么时候会开始离开？

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:13.660
<v Student>I play two varsity sports, volleyball and softball--
<v Student>我参加两项校队运动，排球和垒球——

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:14.290
<v Amie Freak>10:00.
<v Amie Freak>10点。

00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:16.030
<v Student>--again, learning teamwork and dedication.
<v Student>——同样，学习团队合作和奉献。

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:16.480
<v Amie Freak>Yes.
<v Amie Freak>是的。

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:18.190
<v Student>And I hope to obtain scholarships--
<v Student>而且我希望能获得奖学金——

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:21.550
<v Sarah Gibson>What do' you think is going through Angi's head right now?
<v Sarah Gibson>你觉得安吉现在脑子里在想什么？

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:22.600
<v Amie Freak>Hurry the fuck up.
<v Amie Freak>他妈的快点。

00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:24.450
<v Student>--opportunities which this budget cut puts in jeopardy.
<v Student>——这些机会，而这次预算削减危及了这一切。

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:25.780
<v Sarah Gibson>What's going through your head?
<v Sarah Gibson>你脑子里在想什么？

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:35.290
<v Amie Freak>The same thing. I love all these kids. But let's just do this. We all know why we're here. We don't need a boost. We all know.
<v Amie Freak>同一件事。我爱所有这些孩子。但让我们快点吧。我们都知道为什么来这儿。我们不需要催促。我们都知道。

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.980
<v Student>I implore you to support me in having the same opportunities that many of you had.
<v Student>我恳求你们支持我，让我拥有你们许多人拥有的同样机会。

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:50.580
<v Sarah Gibson>A bit later, the other side-- the people who want to stick with the budget cut-- makes their presentation. It's pretty short-- 15 minutes. Toward the end, people applaud early to try to usher the guy off stage.
<v Sarah Gibson>过了一会儿，另一方——那些想坚持预算削减的人——进行了他们的演讲。很短——15分钟。临近结束时，人们提前鼓掌试图把那个人赶下台。

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:58.690
<v Aaron>With that, I will wrap up. And I do have hard copies of this presentation if anybody wants to help circulate these.
<v Aaron>到此为止，我结束了。如果有人想传阅，我确实有这份演讲的纸质复印件。

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:01.660
<v Bruce Jasper>All right, come on. Come on. Come on. Come on.
<v Bruce Jasper>好了，快点。快点。快点。快点。

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:03.130
<v Aaron>I appreciate it, Bruce.
<v Aaron>我领会了，布鲁斯。

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:27.600
<v Sarah Gibson>And finally, 70 minutes in, it's time to vote. People form a long line to put their ballots in the box. It's the OG Croydon ballot box, by the way-- wooden, from the 1890s. Angi's standing in shock, taking this all in. And guess who she sees? Nick Avery, the guy who she and Amie spent half an hour talking with during their door knocking.
<v Sarah Gibson>终于，70分钟过去了，投票时间到了。人们排成长队把选票放入投票箱。顺便说一句，那是克罗伊登的原版投票箱——木制的，1890年代的。安吉震惊地站在那里，观察着这一切。猜猜她看到了谁？尼克·艾弗里，那个她和艾米敲门时聊了半小时的人。

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:34.330
<v Angi Beaulieu>Yes, Nick and his wife and his daughter, who registered today, are here.
<v Angi Beaulieu>是的，尼克和他妻子，还有今天注册的女儿，都在这儿。

00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:35.920
<v Sarah Gibson>Whoa. Were you expecting that?
<v Sarah Gibson>哇。你预料到这一点了吗？

00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:50.650
<v Angi Beaulieu>Absolutely not. Absolutely not. I'm amazed that they came. I feel like the Grinch. My heart has grown three sizes today, Sarah.
<v Angi Beaulieu>绝对没有。绝对没有。我很惊讶他们来了。我觉得自己像那个格林奇。今天我的心长大了三倍，萨拉。

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:56.980
<v Bruce Jasper>All right, I'm seeing nobody else in line that is ready to vote. The voting is now closed.
<v Bruce Jasper>好了，我看到排队的人里没人准备投票了。投票现在结束。

00:39:56.980 --> 00:40:03.640
<v Sarah Gibson>The room starts clearing out quick. Volunteer clerks begin counting votes. Tom is giddy.
<v Sarah Gibson>房间迅速清空。志愿者计票员开始数票。汤姆高兴极了。

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:12.460
<v Tom Moore>Oh, here we go! Here we go! Easy, Ang. Easy, Ang! What's up?
<v Tom Moore>噢，开始啦！开始啦！别急，安吉。别急，安吉！怎么了？

00:40:12.460 --> 00:40:15.400
<v Sarah Gibson>Angi comes up to him. There's a problem.
<v Sarah Gibson>安吉走到他身边。出了问题。

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:21.140
<v Angi Beaulieu>He said we have too many votes. He said we have too many votes.
<v Angi Beaulieu>他说票数太多了。他说我们的票数太多了。

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:22.450
<v Tom Moore>What do you mean we have too many votes?
<v Tom Moore>什么叫票数太多了？

00:40:22.450 --> 00:40:24.850
<v Angi Beaulieu>I don't know.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我不知道。

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:25.660
<v Melanie>What does that mean?
<v Melanie>那是什么意思？

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:26.440
<v Angi Beaulieu>I don't know.
<v Angi Beaulieu>我不知道。

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:28.340
<v Amie Freak>That doesn't make any sense.
<v Amie Freak>那完全说不通。

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:44.530
<v Sarah Gibson>The total number of votes appears to be six more than the number of people who checked in at the door and received ballots. Some people start wondering, was it a counting problem or something sneakier? Maybe the other side slipped in some extra ballots to invalidate the vote.
<v Sarah Gibson>总票数似乎比门口登记领取选票的人数多了六张。有些人开始怀疑，这是统计问题还是更卑鄙的事情？也许另一方塞进了额外的选票来作废投票。

00:40:44.530 --> 00:40:48.290
<v Melanie>Was that their trick? Are you fucking kidding me?
<v Melanie>那是他们的诡计吗？你们在开什么他妈的玩笑吗？

00:40:48.290 --> 00:40:55.930
<v Tom Moore>I don't want people to think that this isn't legitimate. They're going to get on Facebook and say, oh, they cheated.
<v Tom Moore>我不希望人们认为这不是合法的。他们会到Facebook上说，噢，他们作弊了。

00:40:55.930 --> 00:41:03.850
<v Angi Beaulieu>But I don't know what the moderator is going to rule. He may rule we have to vote again. And if that's the case, we don't have 283 people here anymore.
<v Angi Beaulieu>但我不知道主持人会怎么裁定。他可能裁定我们要重新投票。如果是那样，我们就没有283人在现场了。

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:23.950
<v Sarah Gibson>In case anyone needed another reminder that democracy is unpredictable, here we are. The voters who cast their ballots-- some of them have left. And if there's a redo today, it definitely won't make quorum. Meanwhile, the moderator, Bruce, is on the phone with someone from the New Hampshire Secretary of State's office.
<v Sarah Gibson>如果有人还需要提醒民主是不可预测的，我们现在就是。投了票的选民——有些人已经离开了。如果今天要重来一次，肯定达不到法定人数。与此同时，主持人布鲁斯正在和新罕布什尔州州务卿办公室的人通电话。

00:41:23.950 --> 00:41:35.500
<v Tom Moore>Oh my god. I think we might have lost it because of this.
<v Tom Moore>噢，天哪。我觉得我们可能因为这个输了。

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:40.060
<v Sarah Gibson>And then, finally, Bruce is back at the mic.
<v Sarah Gibson>然后，最终，布鲁斯回到了麦克风前。

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:57.910
<v Bruce Jasper>We had a little discrepancy between the number of ballots cast and the number of people that checked in-- voters that checked in. However, that's now been rectified. So the final vote was 377 yes--
<v Bruce Jasper>我们之前的投票数和登记选民数之间有一点差异。不过，现在已经修正了。所以最终投票是赞成377票——

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:04.940
<v Bruce Jasper>[CHEERING]
<v Bruce Jasper>[欢呼]

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:06.170
<v Bruce Jasper>--and two no.
<v Bruce Jasper>——反对2票。

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:08.510
<v Bruce Jasper>[CHEERING]
<v Bruce Jasper>[欢呼]

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:15.740
<v Sarah Gibson>They got what they needed and nearly 100 more. Someone just counted wrong the first time. It's a landslide.
<v Sarah Gibson>他们得到了需要的票数，还多了近100票。有人第一次数错了。这是一场压倒性的胜利。

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:17.910
<v Tom Moore>I take a motion to adjourn.
<v Tom Moore>我提议休会。

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:19.520
<v Woman 1>Mr. Moderator moves to adjourn.
<v Woman 1>主持先生动议休会。

00:42:19.520 --> 00:42:20.210
<v Bruce Jasper>Second?
<v Bruce Jasper>附议？

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:21.330
<v Woman 2>Second!
<v Woman 2>附议！

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:22.950
<v Bruce Jasper>All those in favor--
<v Bruce Jasper>所有赞成的——

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:25.020
<v Tom Moore>Aye!
<v Tom Moore>赞成！

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:27.920
<v Angi Beaulieu>Thank you so much.
<v Angi Beaulieu>非常感谢。

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:32.650
<v Tom Moore>Thank you so much! Oh my god!
<v Tom Moore>非常感谢！噢，天哪！

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:35.420
<v Angi Beaulieu>Thank you so much.
<v Angi Beaulieu>非常感谢。

00:42:35.420 --> 00:43:00.260
<v Sarah Gibson>People are hugging and high-fiving. Angi reaches out to shake hands with a guy she's known for years. They messed with the wrong town, he tells her. They, meaning the budget cutters and the libertarians who championed this. Just a few of them are here, and they don't look happy. We called up Jim Peschke, the guy who spent the morning turkey hunting. He didn't catch one. Here's what he said about the revote.
<v Sarah Gibson>人们在拥抱和击掌。安吉伸出手去和一位认识多年的家伙握手。他告诉她，他们惹错了镇子。他们，指那些预算削减者和支持这一点的自由意志主义者。他们中只有几个人在这儿，看起来很不高兴。我们打电话给了吉姆·佩什克，那个早上花时间去打火鸡的人。他一只没抓到。这是他关于重新投票的评价。

00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:09.870
<v Jim Peschke>It was a very well-run, very effective campaign on their part. I know they did a lot of work. And they were able to get their message through, which I found very surprising.
<v Jim Peschke>这是他们组织的非常出色、非常有效的竞选。我知道他们做了很多工作。他们能够传达他们的信息，这让我非常惊讶。

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:28.700
<v Sarah Gibson>So he accepts the results even though he doesn't agree with them, which, in 2022, is not always something you can count on. And some of the budget cutters-- they're not done. They lost the battle this year but aren't abandoning their mission. Many parents told me that this scare-- it woke them up.
<v Sarah Gibson>所以他接受了结果，尽管他不同意，在2022年，这并不总是可以指望的事情。而且一些预算削减者——他们还没完。他们今年输了，但并没有放弃使命。许多家长告诉我，这次恐吓——它唤醒了他们。

00:43:28.700 --> 00:44:05.620
<v Sarah Gibson>They're continuing to organize, run for office, and show up to meetings. A few days later, the Croydon School Board met. A lot of people came out. And frankly, it was uneventful-- long, tedious. And sitting there, I wondered whether people will keep showing up year after year when there isn't a crisis because, ultimately, how much time are most of us willing to give to democracy?
<v Sarah Gibson>他们继续组织活动，竞选公职，并出现在会议上。几天后，克罗伊登学校董事会开会。很多人出来了。老实说，没发生什么——又长又乏味。坐在那儿，我想知道，当没有危机时，人们还会年复一年地出现吗？因为最终，我们大多数人愿意为民主贡献多少时间？

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:24.820
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Gibson of New Hampshire Public Radio-- that story was produced by Chris Benderev. Coming up, murderous snails tear through an island, leaving a trail of carnage. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>新罕布什尔州公共广播电台的萨拉·吉布森——那个故事由克里斯·本德雷夫制作。接下来，食肉蜗牛撕裂了一个岛屿，留下一片狼藉。芝加哥公共广播电台节目继续。

00:44:24.820 --> 00:44:38.230
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life-- I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "I Work Better on Deadline"-- stories of how deadlines can release an explosion of energy from people who never would have taken action any other way to fix problems that probably should have been addressed long before.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》——我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，“我在最后期限下工作更好”——讲述人们如何因最后期限释放出爆炸性的能量，这些人们在任何其他情况下都不会采取行动去修复那些本应早该处理的问题。

00:44:38.230 --> 00:45:01.910
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Snail in the Coffin. So I think of myself as a not terribly industrious person who happens to have organized his life around a very unforgiving weekly deadline. And really, only because of the deadline, I'm filled with enough terror to finish stories and get a radio show on the air with a coherent beginning, middle, and ending to it, week after week, with a team of people.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，棺材里的蜗牛。所以我认为自己不是一个非常勤奋的人，只是碰巧把生活安排在了一个非常不留情面的每周截止日期周围。说真的，只有因为这个截止日期，我才充满了足够的恐惧去完成故事，并让一个有连贯的开头、中间和结尾的广播节目每周播出，带着一群人。

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:25.340
<v Ira Glass>Without the deadline, I really would never be anywhere as productive. But the stakes of this whole thing are really pretty low, right? If I and my coworkers don't turn out a new show, so what? One less radio episode in the world. Nobody suffers. Nothing dies. The world continues as it was. Not so for the people in this next story. David Kestenbaum explains.
<v Ira Glass>没有截止日期，我真的永远不会像现在这样有效率。但这一切的利害关系其实相当低，对吧？如果我和我的同事没有做出新节目，那又怎样？世界上少了一集广播节目。没人受苦。什么也没死。世界依旧运转。但这对于下一个故事里的人们来说并非如此。大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆解释说。

00:45:25.340 --> 00:45:50.830
<v David Kestenbaum>This is a terrible way to start a story, but it's a fact. I've been kind of obsessed with extinctions recently. Sure, the dinosaurs because who doesn't like a good asteroid extinction? Or maybe it was a comet. There's also the Permian mass extinction, which wiped out over 80% of stuff in the sea-- totally fascinating. I think I'm interested because of this idea that now we may be living through another mass extinction-- one we are causing.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是一个开始故事的糟糕方式，但这是一个事实。我最近一直有点迷恋灭绝。当然，恐龙，因为谁不喜欢好的小行星灭绝呢？或者也许是彗星。还有二叠纪大灭绝，它抹去了海里80%多的生物——非常迷人。我想我是因为这个想法感兴趣，即现在我们可能正在经历另一次大灭绝——一个我们造成的灭绝。

00:45:50.830 --> 00:46:35.980
<v David Kestenbaum>According to the international organization that tracks this stuff, one in eight bird species are threatened. 1/5 of all reptiles, 1/4 of all mammals, and plants-- 40% of all plant species could disappear. 40%-- it's hard to get your head around that. But I sort of feel like I should. Even before this, I did this thing. I regularly tried to imagine my parents' deaths, just to be prepared-- and my own death, which is a kind of personal extinction. It's a way of getting used to the idea of things ending. You can also try to imagine the extinction of democracy. See? It's a fun game.
<v David Kestenbaum>根据追踪这些东西的国际组织，八分之一的鸟类受到威胁。五分之一的爬行动物，四分之一的哺乳动物，还有植物——40%的所有植物物种可能消失。40%——很难让人理解这个数字。但我有点觉得我应该理解。甚至在此之前，我就做过这件事。我经常试着想象我父母的死亡，只是为了做好准备——以及我自己的死亡，这是一种个人的灭绝。这是一种习惯接受事物终结的想法的方法。你也可以试着想象民主的灭绝。看？这是一个有趣的游戏。

00:46:35.980 --> 00:47:18.130
<v David Kestenbaum>This, of course, is an entirely theoretical exercise on my part. But there are people who actually stare into the abyss of it-- of the things facing extinction-- and try to do something. In talking to people, I found this one story I think has never really been told. It's about a snail-- one single species of all these in trouble-- and this chain of really ingenious people who are determined to save it. The story starts with Justin Gerlach, who now is a fellow at Cambridge University. But, in 1992, he was a grad student standing on the island of Raiatea in the middle of the Pacific-- lush tropical place with cliffs-- looking for this snail-- kind of famous in snail circles. It's fancy name-- partula faba.
<v David Kestenbaum>当然，这完全是我个人的理论练习。但有些人确实凝视着它的深渊——那些面临灭绝的事物——并试图做点什么。在与人们交谈时，我发现了一个我认为从未真正被讲述过的故事。它是关于一只蜗牛的——所有这些陷入困境的物种中的一个单一物种——以及这一群决心拯救它的充满创意的人。故事始于贾斯汀·格拉赫，他现在是剑桥大学的研究员。但是，1992年，他是一名研究生，站在太平洋中部赖阿特亚岛上——郁郁葱葱的热带地方，有悬崖——寻找这只蜗牛——在蜗牛圈里有点出名。它花哨的名字——partula faba。

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:21.190
<v Justin Gerlach>I call it Captain Cook's bean snail.
<v Justin Gerlach>我叫它库克船长的豆蜗牛。

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:22.390
<v David Kestenbaum>How come?
<v David Kestenbaum>为什么？

00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:27.400
<v Justin Gerlach>Because it was discovered on Captain Cook's expedition. And faba means bean.
<v Justin Gerlach>因为它是在库克船长的探险中发现的。而faba意思是豆子。

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:28.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Did he call it that?
<v David Kestenbaum>他管它叫那个名字吗？

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:32.200
<v Justin Gerlach>No. No, he didn't call it anything.
<v Justin Gerlach>不。不，他什么也没叫。

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:47.590
<v David Kestenbaum>Sadly but not surprisingly, he says, Captain Cook never wrote anything in his diaries about the snail. It was named afterward. Scientists ended up learning a lot about genetics from partula snails years later. This one-- particularly kind of dignified as snails go-- about an inch long.
<v David Kestenbaum>遗憾但并不令人惊讶的是，他说，库克船长在日记里从未写过关于这只蜗牛的任何事情。它是后来命名的。科学家们多年后最终从帕图拉蜗牛那里学到了很多关于遗传学的知识。这一只——在蜗牛中显得相当庄重——大约一英寸长。

00:47:47.590 --> 00:48:04.690
<v Justin Gerlach>So it's a really nice looking shell. It's the most beautiful of the partula species-- very nice deep yellow color with a dark tip to the shell. And sometimes, they've got dark bands on them as well-- really handsome snail.
<v Justin Gerlach>所以它是一个非常漂亮的壳。它是帕图拉物种中最漂亮的一种——非常漂亮的深黄色，壳尖是深色的。有时它们身上还有深色条纹——非常漂亮的蜗牛。

00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:35.260
<v David Kestenbaum>The snail, when Justin got to the island, was in trouble-- because of us. Justin told me the story with a kind of professorial calm. He's had a front row seat to species extinction for a while now. What happened, he says, is that, at some point, a different snail had been brought to the island-- the giant African land snail-- basically a garden pest, he says. It got out of control. And the solution to that problem was to introduce another snail-- a predatory one that people hoped would eat the African land snail.
<v David Kestenbaum>当贾斯汀到达岛上时，蜗牛陷入了困境——因为我们。贾斯汀带着一种教授式的冷静讲述了这个故事。他有一段时间一直坐在物种灭绝的第一线。他说，发生的事情是，在某个时候，另一种蜗牛被带到了岛上——大非洲陆地蜗牛——基本上是一种园艺害虫，他说。它失控了。解决那个问题的办法是引入另一种蜗牛——一种捕食性的蜗牛，人们希望它会吃掉非洲陆地蜗牛。

00:48:35.260 --> 00:48:39.780
<v David Kestenbaum>It's kind of crazy that they had one out-of-control control snail, so they introduced a second snail--
<v David Kestenbaum>他们有一种失控的控制蜗牛，所以他们引入了第二种蜗牛——

00:48:39.780 --> 00:48:40.840
<v Justin Gerlach>Yep.
<v Justin Gerlach>是的。

00:48:40.840 --> 00:48:42.440
<v David Kestenbaum>--to eat the first snail.
<v David Kestenbaum>——去吃掉第一种蜗牛。

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:43.540
<v Justin Gerlach>Yep.
<v Justin Gerlach>是的。

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:47.590
<v David Kestenbaum>Maybe you can see where this is going. The new snail had no interest in this assignment.
<v David Kestenbaum>也许你能看出这要走向何方。新来的蜗牛对这项任务毫无兴趣。

00:48:47.590 --> 00:48:59.770
<v Justin Gerlach>Basically, it didn't like the giant African snail. They're too big. They're too leathery. So it doesn't eat them. It likes the little forest snails instead.
<v Justin Gerlach>基本上，它不喜欢大非洲蜗牛。它们太大了。它们太像皮革了。所以它不吃它们。它反而喜欢小森林蜗牛。

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:29.460
<v David Kestenbaum>Including Captain Cook's bean snail. Climate change, for sure, is putting a lot of species in danger. But most creatures on the endangered list-- they're there for reasons like this-- much more basic stuff. We cut down some forest to farm or build homes. We catch too many fish. Or we bring some foreign snail to an island. So Justin's on the island to rescue any survivors he can find. He heads out and gets to this one valley where the invading snails had apparently just swept through, maybe a week before.
<v David Kestenbaum>包括库克船长的豆蜗牛。气候变化确实让很多物种处于危险之中。但濒危名单上的大多数生物——它们出现在那里是因为这样的原因——更基本的东西。我们砍掉一些森林来耕种或盖房。我们捕捞过多的鱼。或者我们带一些外来蜗牛到岛上。所以贾斯汀在岛上营救他能找到的幸存者。他出发并到达了这个山谷，入侵的蜗牛显然就在一周前刚刚席卷过这里。

00:49:29.460 --> 00:49:39.390
<v Justin Gerlach>And it was just completely littered with hundreds of empty shells. And some of them were still damp from where they'd been eaten by the predatory snails.
<v Justin Gerlach>那里完全满地都是数百个空壳。其中一些甚至还是湿的，就是它们被捕食性蜗牛吃掉的地方。

00:49:39.390 --> 00:49:40.920
<v David Kestenbaum>He had this thought.
<v David Kestenbaum>他有了这个念头。

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:45.750
<v Justin Gerlach>Maybe, actually, I've been too late-- just that little bit too late.
<v Justin Gerlach>也许，事实上，我来晚了——仅仅是晚了那么一点点。

00:49:45.750 --> 00:49:47.820
<v David Kestenbaum>There's like a massacre or something.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就像是一场屠杀什么的。

00:49:47.820 --> 00:49:53.550
<v Justin Gerlach>Yeah, very much a massacre-- a slow-motion massacre but, nonetheless--
<v Justin Gerlach>是的，非常像屠杀——一场慢动作的屠杀，但尽管如此——

00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:58.470
<v David Kestenbaum>That's snails are slow-- joke number one for this story. There'll be another.
<v David Kestenbaum>蜗牛很慢——这个故事的第一个笑话。还会有另一个。

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:02.730
<v David Kestenbaum>How can they be so good at killing every last snail?
<v David Kestenbaum>它们怎么能如此擅长杀掉每一只蜗牛？

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:22.410
<v Justin Gerlach>They're extremely efficient predators. So they're very good at picking up the chemical cues left behind by the slime trails. So they track them down like a bloodhound, essentially. And then they're very good, perfectly evolved killing machines, really.
<v Justin Gerlach>它们是非常有效的捕食者。它们非常擅长捕捉粘液痕迹留下的化学信号。所以它们基本上像猎犬一样追踪它们。然后它们是非常完美进化的杀戮机器，真的。

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:56.310
<v David Kestenbaum>If it seems hard to imagine how an animal with no arms or hands or claws or talons, whose name is appropriately synonymous with slowness, can kill anything at all, I found a video online. It's gruesome. The predatory snail just kind of sucks the other snail out of its shell while it struggles to fight back. Justin does find one-- one bean snail in that valley. He puts it in a plastic container. And then a piece of luck-- he finds an old vanilla plantation the predatory snails hadn't reached yet. And there are dozens of bean snails.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果很难想象一只没有手臂、手、爪子或指甲的动物，名字恰当地与缓慢同义，竟然能杀掉任何东西，我在网上找到了一个视频。它非常恐怖。捕食性蜗牛只是把另一只蜗牛从壳里吸出来，同时它挣扎反抗。贾斯汀在那山谷里找到了一只——一只豆蜗牛。他把它放进一个塑料容器里。然后运气来了——他发现了一个古老的香草种植园，捕食性蜗牛还没到达那里。那里有几十只豆蜗牛。

00:50:56.310 --> 00:50:58.050
<v David Kestenbaum>You got there just in time.
<v David Kestenbaum>你及时赶到了。

00:50:58.050 --> 00:51:06.040
<v Justin Gerlach>Just in time, yes. It must have been weeks or months before that valley got eliminated.
<v Justin Gerlach>及时，是的。那个山谷被消灭肯定是在几周或几个月前。

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:37.900
<v David Kestenbaum>And with that, Justin found himself in a remarkable position. He had some of the very last of a species that had been around for a million years-- the last on earth. After Justin collected the snails, he packed them up and took them on a plane. The snails rode in the overhead bin. He checked them from time to time. The snails that Justin collected, along with some others that had been gathered the previous year, were sent to a handful of places that could care for them.
<v David Kestenbaum>就这样，贾斯汀发现自己处于一个非凡的位置。他手里有一些已经存在了百万年的物种的最后一些成员——地球上的最后一些。贾斯汀收集蜗牛后，把它们装好带上了飞机。蜗牛坐在头顶行李架上。他时常检查它们。贾斯汀收集的蜗牛，以及前一年收集的一些蜗牛，被送往几个能照看它们的地方。

00:51:37.900 --> 00:51:54.250
<v David Kestenbaum>The plan was simple. If you could get them to breed, then you go from tens of snails to hundreds, thousands. And you could put them back in nature. Some of the snails were sent to the Bristol Zoo. Visitors would walk right by them-- no idea they were looking at what could be the last of their kind on earth.
<v David Kestenbaum>计划很简单。如果你能让它们繁殖，那么你就从几十只蜗牛变成数百只、数千只。你可以把它们放回大自然。一些蜗牛被送到了布里斯托尔动物园。游客们会直接走过它们——完全不知道他们正在看的是地球上最后的同类。

00:51:54.250 --> 00:52:12.430
<v David Kestenbaum>Melissa Bushell was one of the keepers in charge of them, in the hopes that their numbers would increase-- and, I have to say, kind of the perfect person to try to pull this thing off-- dedicated, methodical, and, aside from a detour as a bartender, she can trace this career choice way back. Melissa recently transitioned, by the way. She asked that we share that.
<v David Kestenbaum>梅丽莎·布舍尔是负责照顾它们的饲养员之一，希望它们的数量能增加——而且，我必须说，是尝试完成这件事的完美人选——敬业、有条理，除了曾有一段时间当过酒保的插曲，她可以追溯这个职业选择到很久以前。顺便说一句，梅丽莎最近完成了跨性别转换。她要求我们分享这一点。

00:52:12.430 --> 00:52:26.580
<v Melissa Bushell>Yeah, since I was about, maybe-- I don't know-- probably about two or three or four, I just always liked bugs and reptiles and fish and animals that weren't fluffy, basically. So yeah, it's always been a bit of a passion.
<v Melissa Bushell>是的，从我大约，可能——我不知道——可能大约两三四岁开始，我一直喜欢虫子、爬行动物、鱼和那些不毛茸茸的动物，基本上就是这样。所以是的，这一直是一种热情。

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:28.240
<v David Kestenbaum>Wait, how come not fluffy?
<v David Kestenbaum>等等，为什么不是毛茸茸的？

00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:40.150
<v Melissa Bushell>Well, everyone else liked the fluffy things like monkeys and tigers. And I've kind of felt bad for the bugs and the things that people thought were a bit ick. So, you know, they needed somebody to like them as well.
<v Melissa Bushell>嗯，其他人都喜欢毛茸茸的东西，比如猴子和老虎。我一直有点为虫子和那些人们觉得有点恶心的东西感到遗憾。所以，你知道，它们也需要有人喜欢它们。

00:52:40.150 --> 00:53:01.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Melissa, usually listening to classical music on the radio, would tend to the snails, feed them twice a week a mixture of porridge oats, trout food pellet, and cuddle bone ground into a powder, change out the sheet of plastic wrap that covers the top of the glass boxes they were in, and see if their numbers would increase. Melissa spent a lot of time watching them. There's a little dance they do when they mate.
<v David Kestenbaum>梅丽莎通常听着收音机里的古典音乐，每周给蜗牛喂两次燕麦片、鳟鱼饲料颗粒和磨成粉的墨鱼骨，更换覆盖在它们所在的玻璃盒顶部的保鲜膜，看看它们的数量是否会增加。梅丽莎花了很多时间看着它们。它们交配时有一个小小的舞蹈。

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:03.070
<v Melissa Bushell>Oh, it's super cute to watch.
<v Melissa Bushell>噢，看着它们非常可爱。

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:05.740
<v David Kestenbaum>What's the dance like?
<v David Kestenbaum>那舞蹈是什么样的？

00:53:05.740 --> 00:53:08.560
<v Melissa Bushell>Very slow. [LAUGHS]
<v Melissa Bushell>非常慢。[笑]

00:53:08.560 --> 00:53:09.670
<v David Kestenbaum>Told you.
<v David Kestenbaum>跟你说了吧。

00:53:09.670 --> 00:53:22.720
<v Melissa Bushell>They kind of just end up sort of circling each other. And I'm sure each species does it slightly differently. But then one of them will sort of climb on next to the other one. And then they will touch their necks together because that's where their genitalia is. And they'll sort of mate that way.
<v Melissa Bushell>它们最后只是围着对方绕圈。我相信每个物种的做法略有不同。但之后其中一个会爬到另一个旁边。然后它们会把脖子贴在一起，因为那是它们的生殖器所在的地方。它们会那样交配。

00:53:22.720 --> 00:53:24.070
<v David Kestenbaum>Genitalia is on the neck?
<v David Kestenbaum>生殖器在脖子上？

00:53:24.070 --> 00:53:25.210
<v Melissa Bushell>Yeah.
<v Melissa Bushell>是的。

00:53:25.210 --> 00:53:26.650
<v David Kestenbaum>They really are like an alien species.
<v David Kestenbaum>它们确实像一个外星物种。

00:53:26.650 --> 00:53:32.740
<v Melissa Bushell>Mm-hmm, that's why I like invertebrates so much. They're just weird but good.
<v Melissa Bushell>嗯，这就是为什么我这么喜欢无脊椎动物。它们很怪，但很好。

00:53:32.740 --> 00:53:47.410
<v David Kestenbaum>Then, a few weeks later, a baby would be born-- just the size of a period you might write with a pencil at the end of a sentence. So the breeding was working. But there was a problem.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后，几周后，一个宝宝就会出生——只有你在句子末尾用铅笔写下的句号那么大。所以繁殖起作用了。但有一个问题。

00:53:47.410 --> 00:54:15.970
<v Melissa Bushell>Give me a second. I can tell you exactly how many we had. So when I started, we had 80 of them. We went from 80 at the end of 2010 to 60 in March 2011, 61 in June 2011. September 2011, 52.
<v Melissa Bushell>给我一秒钟。我可以准确告诉你我们有多少。我刚开始的时候，我们有80只。我们从2010年底的80只变成了2011年3月的60只，2011年6月的61只。2011年9月，52只。

00:54:15.970 --> 00:54:36.070
<v David Kestenbaum>The snails were reproducing but not fast enough to replace the ones that were dying. No one could figure out why. I want to be sure you get a picture of just how much effort and expertise went into this. It was a whole team of people. They did autopsies of each snail when it died, taking them out of their shells, preserving them, slicing them, looking at them under a microscope.
<v David Kestenbaum>蜗牛在繁殖，但速度不足以替代死去的那些。没人能查出原因。我想确保你能了解在这个项目上投入了多少努力和专业知识。那是一整个团队的人。每只蜗牛死亡时他们都会进行尸检，把它从壳里取出来，保存，切片，在显微镜下观察。

00:54:36.070 --> 00:55:03.250
<v David Kestenbaum>They even reached out to Justin, who'd rescued the last ones on the island as a grad student. Justin, thinking maybe there was a problem with the diet, went back to an old museum collection that had snails preserved from over 100 years ago. Surprisingly, he found ants in some of their stomachs. But they determined the snails probably weren't feeding on ants. The snails probably had just eaten fruit that had ants in it. No one had any idea what was wrong. Their numbers continued to drop without explanation.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们甚至联系了贾斯汀，他作为研究生营救了岛上最后的一些蜗牛。贾斯汀思考也许饮食有问题，回到了一个有一百多年历史的老博物馆收藏中，那里有保存的蜗牛。令人惊讶的是，他在其中一些胃里发现了蚂蚁。但他们断定蜗牛可能并没有以蚂蚁为食。蜗牛可能只是吃了有蚂蚁的水果。没人知道出了什么问题。它们的数量在没有解释的情况下继续下降。

00:55:03.250 --> 00:55:06.740
<v Melissa Bushell>By May 2014, we were down to 17.
<v Melissa Bushell>到2014年5月，我们剩下17只了。

00:55:06.740 --> 00:55:07.510
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh.
<v David Kestenbaum>噢。

00:55:07.510 --> 00:55:11.560
<v Melissa Bushell>Yeah. Then June-- 14--
<v Melissa Bushell>是的。然后六月——14只——

00:55:11.560 --> 00:55:12.730
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh.
<v David Kestenbaum>噢。

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:24.280
<v Melissa Bushell>--and then down to 9 on the 16th of September 2014. And then that's when they went to Edinburgh.
<v Melissa Bushell>——然后到2014年9月16日剩下9只。然后那时它们去了爱丁堡。

00:55:24.280 --> 00:55:52.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Edinburgh Zoo. If there's anywhere that could save them, it was Edinburgh. If the president were sick, that's where you'd send him, if he were a snail. Years before this, Edinburgh Zoo had been sent a single snail-- the very last of its kind in captivity-- just one-- a subspecies called Partula taeniata simulans. They kept it alive. And somehow, it was pregnant. It gave birth. And soon, there were hundreds and hundreds of them. And they were put back in the wild.
<v David Kestenbaum>爱丁堡动物园。如果有什么地方能救它们，那就是爱丁堡。如果总统病了，你会把他送到那儿，如果他是一只蜗牛的话。在这之前很多年，爱丁堡动物园收到过一只蜗牛——俘获中仅存的最后一只同类——只有一只——一个叫Partula taeniata simulans的亚种。它们让它活了下来。不知怎么的，它怀孕了。它生下了后代。很快，就有成百上千只。它们被放回了野外。

00:55:52.910 --> 00:56:34.240
<v David Kestenbaum>Melissa was full of worry, though-- said, when the last bean snails went up to Edinburgh Zoo in a van, it felt like handing off a grenade that the pin had just been taken out of. The situation these people were in with the bean snail, where you know you have the last ones in your hands-- it's very rare. More often, we just don't know if something's going extinct. Usually, scientists suspect something in the wild has disappeared. But there's a long process of searching and surveying to determine, yes, it's really gone. The standard is, when there's no reasonable doubt, the last individual of a species has died.
<v David Kestenbaum>梅丽莎充满了担忧，她说，当最后一些豆蜗牛被面包车送到爱丁堡动物园时，感觉就像递出了一颗拔掉了销的手榴弹。这些人带着豆蜗牛所处的这种境地，你知道手里拿着最后一些——非常罕见。通常，我们只是不知道某种东西是否正在灭绝。通常，科学家怀疑野外的某种东西已经消失了。但有一个漫长的搜索和调查过程来确定，是的，它真的消失了。标准是，当没有合理怀疑时，物种的最后一个个体已经死亡。

00:56:34.240 --> 00:57:12.870
<v David Kestenbaum>This year, six new things were officially designated as extinct-- the Chinese paddlefish, three Moroccan freshwater fish, a tree from Asia, and a fly from the UK, last seen in 1907. But they weren't there yet with the bean snail. The nine remaining snails arrived at Edinburgh Zoo. The snails were not put on display but were kept instead in a quiet room-- an old coal shed, actually, where that other single snail had come back from the brink. Ross Poulter, who helped take care of them, told me he thinks the light there is perfectly dim, like it would be in the forest. He said he felt a kind of awe being in there with them.
<v David Kestenbaum>今年，六个新物种被正式指定为灭绝——长江白鲟、三种摩洛哥淡水鱼、一种来自亚洲的树，以及一种自1907年以来最后一次见到、来自英国的苍蝇。但他们还没到豆蜗牛这一步。剩下的九只蜗牛到达了爱丁堡动物园。蜗牛没有被展出，而是被养在一个安静的房间里——其实是一个旧煤棚，也就是那只曾从边缘回来的单只蜗牛待过的地方。参与照顾它们的罗斯·波尔特告诉我，他认为那里的光线非常暗，就像森林里一样。他说他待在它们身边时感到一种敬畏。

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:28.740
<v Ross Poulter>You know, you can be in that room on your own, looking after these snails. And you peel back the cling film on the lid to reach in, and you're actually feeding the last individuals. That's it.
<v Ross Poulter>你知道，你可以在那个房间里独自照顾这些蜗牛。你揭开盖子上的保鲜膜伸手进去，你喂养的其实是最后的一些个体。就是这样。

00:57:28.740 --> 00:58:11.090
<v David Kestenbaum>He wondered, what would it be like if you had the last elephant? Within a year, eight of the snails had died, which meant they were down to one snail-- the last bean snail. Some snails can reproduce without a mate-- basically impregnate themselves. Every time he went in there, Ross would check to see if there was a baby. A month went by-- this one snail just doing its thing-- no baby. Two months, three months, six months.
<v David Kestenbaum>他想，如果你拥有最后一只大象会是什么感觉？一年内，八只蜗牛死亡，这意味着只剩下一只——最后一只豆蜗牛。有些蜗牛没有伴侣也能繁殖——基本上自我受孕。每次进去，罗斯都会查看是否有宝宝。一个月过去了——这只蜗牛只是在做它自己的事——没有宝宝。两个月，三个月，六个月。

00:58:11.090 --> 00:58:27.330
<v Ross Poulter>I remember when I went in there to do my morning checks, and it was quite clearly dead. And I do remember just standing there looking at it, thinking, it's actually very sad, you know? It was quite thought provoking.
<v Ross Poulter>我记得当我进去做早晨检查时，它明显死了。我确实记得只是站在那里看着它，心想，这真的非常悲伤，你知道吗？这非常发人深省。

00:58:27.330 --> 00:58:38.220
<v David Kestenbaum>He thought about how the famous British explorer, Captain James Cook, had been there at the beginning. And now, at the other end, him, Ross Poulter, looking at the last one.
<v David Kestenbaum>他想到了著名的英国探险家詹姆斯·库克船长在开始时就在那里。现在，在另一端，他，罗斯·波尔特，看着最后一只。

00:58:38.220 --> 00:58:45.430
<v Ross Poulter>So you have all those kind of thoughts. And I was in there on my own. And nobody else at that moment knew about it.
<v Ross Poulter>所以你有那种想法。我当时独自在里面。那时没人知道这件事。

00:58:45.430 --> 00:58:56.310
<v Jo Elliott>I still remember vividly the day that they brought that last snail up in a little alcohol tube to preserve it for posterity. And just everyone was heartbroken.
<v Jo Elliott>我依然生动地记得那天他们把最后一只蜗牛放在一个小酒精管里带上来，为了后代保留它。每个人都心碎了。

00:58:56.310 --> 00:58:59.370
<v David Kestenbaum>This is Jo Elliott, who was overseeing things.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是乔·艾略特，他负责监督这件事。

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:12.550
<v Jo Elliott>I mean, at the time, you get on with it. You're professional. You deal with it. But that snail-- I kept it on my desk for a long time as a reminder of what it is we're trying to do and what we're trying to avoid.
<v Jo Elliott>我的意思是，当时，你只能继续下去。你很专业。你处理它。但那只蜗牛——我把它放在桌子上很长一段时间，作为一个提醒，提醒我们正在努力做什么，以及我们正在努力避免什么。

00:59:12.550 --> 00:59:18.060
<v David Kestenbaum>Was that the first thing you'd worked with that you'd actually watched to go extinct?
<v David Kestenbaum>那是你参与过的第一件你真正亲眼看着灭绝的东西吗？

00:59:18.060 --> 00:59:28.580
<v Jo Elliott>Yep that's the only thing I've worked with that I've seen go extinct in that way. It's quite a life-changing experience.
<v Jo Elliott>是的，这是我参与过的唯一一件以那种方式看着灭绝的东西。这是一个相当改变人生的经历。

00:59:28.580 --> 00:59:33.410
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, I feel like, you know, it says something about our species that we're in this position in the first place.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，我觉得，你知道，这说明我们物种本身就处于这个位置。

00:59:33.410 --> 00:59:38.760
<v Jo Elliott>Yeah, absolutely, because this was an entirely man-made situation.
<v Jo Elliott>是的，绝对，因为这完全是人为造成的情况。

00:59:38.760 --> 00:59:43.380
<v David Kestenbaum>But it also says something that there are so many people trying so hard to keep them alive.
<v David Kestenbaum>但这也说明有很多很多人在努力让它们活下去。

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:49.990
<v Jo Elliott>Absolutely. Yeah. Yeah, both sides of the coin.
<v Jo Elliott>绝对。是的。是的，硬币的两面。

00:59:49.990 --> 01:00:21.860
<v David Kestenbaum>As far as I can tell, the end of the snail that had been around for a million years-- other than a handful of people, nobody noticed-- no stories in the papers or online. I'd wanted to know what it was like to come face to face with extinction. Ross knew. When the last one died, Ross was the one who put it in the glass jar. It's his neat handwriting on the side that says, P Faba, for partula faba. Last known individual, he wrote. I asked if he'd managed to absorb what had happened.
<v David Kestenbaum>据我所知，这种已经存在了百万年的蜗牛的终结——除了少数人，没人注意到——报纸或网上没有报道。我一直想知道面对灭绝是什么感觉。罗斯知道。最后一只死的时候，罗斯就是那个把它放进玻璃罐的人。侧面是他整洁的字迹，写着“P Faba”，代表partula faba。他写道，“最后已知的个体”。我问他是否已经消化了发生的事情。

01:00:21.860 --> 01:00:48.530
<v David Kestenbaum>He said, I don't know how to answer that. Even when you see it up close, it's hard to understand. It's too big. It's not on our time scale at all. You'd have to somehow be able to imagine snails being born one generation after another for, like, a million years and then it stopping-- in Ross' case, on some Sunday at the office before your very eyes. What are you supposed to do after that?
<v David Kestenbaum>他说，我不知道该怎么回答。即使你近距离看到它，也很难理解。它太大了。这根本不在我们的时间尺度上。你必须以某种方式想象蜗牛一代又一代地出生，持续一百万年，然后停止——在罗斯的情况下，在某个星期天的办公室里，就在你眼前。那之后你应该做什么？

01:00:48.530 --> 01:01:17.780
<v David Kestenbaum>We're so used to stories about people on deadline facing one last chance to save things and figuring out some way at the last moment. But I think the truth is when you get to the point where things are so desperate, they are down to one last chance, it's rarely a good one. I know. That's a terrible way to end a story. But it's a fact.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们习惯了关于在截止日期前的人们面临最后机会拯救事物，并在最后一刻找到某种方法的报道。但我认为真相是，当你到达情况如此绝望的地步，只剩下最后一次机会时，它很少会是好结果。我知道。这是一个糟糕的结束故事的方式。但这是一个事实。

01:01:17.780 --> 01:02:01.910
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is our show's Senior Editor.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的高级编辑。

01:02:01.910 --> 01:02:20.310
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced by Sean Cole. The people who put together today's program include Elna Baker, Michael Comite, Valerie Kipnis, Miki Meek, Katherine Rae Mondo, Michelle Navarro, Stowe Nelson, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Charlotte Sleeper, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Diane Wu. Our Managing Editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our Executive Editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目是由肖恩·科尔制作的。今天共同制作节目的还有艾尔娜·贝克、迈克尔·科米特、瓦莱丽·基普尼斯、三木·米克、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、米歇尔·纳瓦罗、斯托·尼尔森、纳迪亚·雷曼、瑞安·鲁梅里、夏洛特·斯利珀、弗朗西斯·斯旺森、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉、马特·蒂尔尼和黛安·吴。我们的执行编辑是莎拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼。我们的执行主编是埃马努埃莱·贝里。

01:02:20.310 --> 01:02:45.330
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Maria Hollenhorst, Craig Hilton-Taylor, Justine Paradis, Jack Rodolico, Katie Colaneri, Ed Spiker, Dan Barrick, Maureen McMurray, Stephen Andersen, Susan Solomon, Robert Watson, Joseph Steed, and Jim Baker. Our website is thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 750 episodes for absolutely free. Also, there are videos. There are lists of favorite shows if you're looking for something to listen to and tons of other stuff there. Again, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢今天的玛丽亚·霍伦霍斯特、克雷格·希尔顿-泰勒、贾斯汀·帕拉迪斯、杰克·罗多利科、凯蒂·科拉内里、埃德·斯派克、丹·巴里克、莫琳·麦克默里、斯蒂芬·安徒生、苏珊·所罗门、罗伯特·沃森、约瑟夫·斯蒂德和吉姆·贝克。我们的网站是thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以绝对免费地在线收听我们超过750集的档案。此外，还有视频。如果你正在寻找可以听的东西，还有喜欢的节目列表以及大量其他东西。再次说明，thisamericanlife.org。

01:02:45.330 --> 01:03:06.750
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, we were at the car rental place the other day. We waited in line forever. The rental people were so rude. Finally, Torey just lost it. He said, we're going somewhere else-- Avis, Enterprise, Hertz-- anyplace but this place.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》通过公共广播交流中心PRX发送给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚先生。你知道，前几天我们在汽车租赁处。我们排队等了很久。租赁的人非常粗鲁。最后，托雷崩溃了。他说，我们要去别的地方——AVIS、企业、赫兹——除了这儿哪儿都行。

01:03:06.750 --> 01:03:09.720
<v Torey Malatia>This Budget just does not work.
<v Torey Malatia>这份预算根本行不通。

01:03:09.720 --> 01:03:14.720
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来，带着更多《美国生活》的故事。
